<introduction>
<p>The following topics will be covered in this course.</p>
<p><alert>(i)</alert>  Measure theory and Lebesgue integrals (first six or seven weeks).</p>
<p><alert>Q: Why do we need Lebesgue integrals?</alert></p>
<p>Recall the Riemann integral:
Let <m>f \colon [a,b] \subset \R \to \R</m> be a bounded function. For any partition
<md>P \coloneqq a = x_0 &lt; x_1 &lt; \ldots &lt; x_n = b \text{ of } [a,b],</md>
consider
<md>
<mrow>U(f,P) = \sum_{j=1}^{n}\sup\set{f(x)\mid x_{j-1}\le x\le x_{j}}(x_{j}-x_{j-1}),</mrow>
<mrow>L(f,P) = \sum_{j=1}^{n}\inf\set{f(x)\mid x_{j-1}\le x\le x_{j}}(x_{j}-x_{j-1}).</mrow>
</md>
Define
<md>\overline{\int_{a}^{b}} f(x) \,dx = \inf_{P} U(f,P), \quad
        \underline{\int_{a}^{b}} f(x) \,dx = \sup_{P} L(f,P).</md>
We say that <m>f</m> is <em>Riemann integrable</em> if
<md>\overline{\int_{a}^{b}} f(x) \,dx = \underline{\int_{a}^{b}} f(x) \,dx.</md></p>
<example xml:id="ex-dirichlet-function">
<p>Let <m>f_\Q\colon [a,b]\to\R</m> be defined by
<md>
<mrow>f_\Q(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            0 &amp; \text{if } x\notin\Q, \\
            1 &amp; \text{if } x\in\Q.
        \end{cases}</mrow>
</md>
In this case,
<md>\overline{\int_{a}^{b}}f_\Q(x) \,dx = b-a, \quad
        \underline{\int_{a}^{b}}f_\Q(x) \,dx = 0,</md>
so <m>f_\Q</m> is not Riemann integrable.</p>
</example>
<p>Lebesgue's observation is that <m>f</m> should be Riemann integrable if continuity is abundant.
Lebesgue's theorem states that <m>f</m> is Riemann integrable if and only if the set
<md>\set{x\in[a,b]\mid f\text{ is not continuous at }x}</md>
has measure zero.
Lebesgue proposed another approach to integration:
Let <m>f\colon [a,b]\subset\R\to\R</m> be a bounded function.
Consider
<md>\alpha \coloneqq \inf\set{f(x)\mid x\in[a,b]}, \quad
        \beta \coloneqq \sup\set{f(x)\mid x\in[a,b]}.</md>
Let <m>Q\coloneqq \alpha=y_0&lt;y_1&lt;\ldots&lt;y_n=\beta</m> be a partition of <m>[\alpha,\beta]</m>.
For <m>j=1,2,\ldots,n</m>, put
<md>A_j = \set{x\in[a,b]\mid y_{j-1}&lt;f(x)\le y_{j}}, \quad
        A_0 = \set{x\in[a,b]\mid f(x)=\alpha}.</md>
Let <m>y_{-1}=\alpha</m> and define
<md>U_{Q} = \sum_{j=0}^{n} y_{j} m(A_j), \quad
        L_{Q} = \sum_{j=0}^{n} y_{j-1} m(A_j),</md>
where <m>m(A_j)</m> denotes the measure of <m>A_j</m>.
Let
<md>\mathrm{Le}\overline{\int_{a}^{b}}f(x)\,dx  = \inf_{Q} U_Q, \quad
        \mathrm{Le}\underline{\int_{a}^{b}}f(x)\,dx  = \sup_{Q} L_Q.</md>
We say that <m>f</m> is <em>Lebesgue integrable</em> if
<md>\mathrm{Le}\overline{\int_{a}^{b}}f(x)\,dx = \mathrm{Le}\underline{\int_{a}^{b}}f(x)\,dx.</md></p>
<p>Let us go back to the example of <m>f_\Q</m>.
In this case, one has
<md>A_j = \set{x\in [a,b]\mid y_{j-1}&lt;f(x)\le y_j} = \varnothing</md>
for <m>i=1,2,\ldots,n-1</m>, and
<md>A_0 = \R\setminus \Q, \quad A_n = \Q.</md>
Since <m>m(A_0)=1</m> and <m>m(A_j)=0</m> for <m>j=1,2,\ldots,n</m>, it follows that
<md>\mathrm{Le}\overline{\int_{a}^{b}}f(x)\,dx = \mathrm{Le}\underline{\int_{a}^{b}}f(x)\,dx = 0.</md></p>
<p>In view of this, the problem of defining integrals is reduced to the problem of defining  measures.
There are three main questions in Lebesgue integral theory: What is <m>m(A_j)</m>? For what <m>f</m> is <m>m(A_j)</m> defined? Is this better than Riemann integrals?</p>
<p><alert>(ii)</alert>  Distribution theory and fourier analysis. The latter will be covered in the next semester.</p>
<p>Recall the following exercise from calculus:
Let <m>f(x), g(x)\colon [0,1]\subset\R\to\R</m> be two continuous functions.
Suppose
<md>\int_{a}^{b}f(x)\phi(x)\,dx = \int_{a}^{b}g(x)\phi(x)\,dx</md>
for every continuous function <m>\phi\colon[0,1]\subset\R\to\R</m>.
Show that <m>f(x)=g(x)</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>.</p>
<p>We may regard a function <m>f\colon[0,1]\subset\R\to\R</m> as a linear transformation
<md>
<mrow>f\colon
        \begin{array}{ccc}
        C_c^\infty(\R) &amp; \to &amp; \R \\
        \phi &amp; \mapsto &amp; \int_{\R}f(x)\phi(x)\,dx
        \end{array},</mrow>
</md>
where <m>C_c^\infty(\R)</m> denotes the space of infinitely differentiable functions with compact support in <m>\R</m>.</p>
<p>A <em>distribution</em> is a continuous linear transformation
<md>
<mrow>T\colon
        \begin{array}{ccc}
            C_c^\infty &amp; \to &amp; \R \\
            \phi &amp; \mapsto &amp; T(\phi)
        \end{array}.</mrow>
</md>
One advantage of distributions is that they are infinitely differentiable in some sense.
The derivative of a distribution is, again, a distribution
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\pr T}{\pr x_j}\colon
        \begin{array}{ccc}
            C_c^\infty &amp; \to &amp; \R \\
            \phi &amp; \mapsto &amp; -T(\frac{\pr\phi}{\pr x_j})
        \end{array}.</mrow>
</md>
This definition is motivated by integration by parts: Suppose <m>T</m> is given by a differentiable function <m>f</m>, then
<md>\left(\frac{\pr T}{\pr x_j}\right)(\phi)
        = \int_{\R^n} \frac{\pr f}{\pr x_j}\phi\,dx
        = -\int_{\R^n} f(x)\frac{\pr \phi}{\pr x_j}\,dx
        = -T\left(\frac{\pr \phi}{\pr x_j}\right).</md>
However, <m>f</m> needs not be continuous in the definition of distributions.</p>
<p>A famous example is Dirac's delta function:
<md>
<mrow>\delta\colon
        \begin{array}{ccc}
            C_c^\infty(\R^n) &amp; \to &amp; \R \\
            f(x) &amp; \mapsto &amp; f(0)
        \end{array}.</mrow>
</md></p>
<p>Here is another example.
Let <m>\Omega=\set{x\in\R^n\mid r(x)&lt;0}</m> be a bounded domain, where <m>r\in C^\infty(\R^n)</m> and <m>\nabla r=(\frac{\pr r}{\pr x_1},\ldots,\frac{\pr r}{\pr x_n})\ne 0</m> on <m>\pr\Omega=\set{x\in\R^n\mid r(x)=0}</m>.
Then
<md>
<mrow>1_\Omega\colon
        \begin{array}{ccc}
            C_c^\infty(\R^n) &amp; \to &amp; \R \\
            \phi &amp; \mapsto &amp; 1_{\Omega}(\phi) = \int_\Omega\phi(x)\,dx
        \end{array}</mrow>
</md>
is a distribution.
This is the distributional manifestation of the identity function
<md>
<mrow>1_\Omega(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            1 &amp; \text{if } x\in\Omega \\
            0 &amp; \text{if } x\notin\Omega
        \end{cases}.</mrow>
</md>
The derivative of this distribution can be found using the Gauss-Green theorem:
<md>\frac{\pr 1_\Omega}{\pr x_j}(\phi)
        = -1_\Omega\left(\frac{\pr \phi}{\pr x_j}\right)
        = -\int_{\Omega}\frac{\pr \phi}{\pr x_j}\,dx
        = \int_{\pr\Omega} \phi N_j \,dS,</md>
where <m>N=(N_1,N_2)</m> is the normal vector pointing outward.
Hence, <m>\frac{\pr 1_\Omega}{\pr x_j}=N_j\,dS</m>.</p>
<p>Distribution theory is the foundation of modern analysis, including but not limited to functional analysis, harmonic analysis, and partial differential equations (PDE).
A common theme in the research of PDE is to find the distributional solutions to an equation first and then study the smoothness (regularity) of such solutions.</p>
<aside>
<title>Supplement</title>
<p>The existence of distributional solutions to the Navier-Stokes equation has been known since the work of Jean Leray in 1934.
(During World War II, while imprisoned, Leray also made significant contributions to algebraic topology and homological algebra.)
However, the question of whether theses solutions remain smooth has persisted for more than 90 years.
Just a few days ago, some claimed to have solved the Navier-Stokes problem, constructing a solution that is smooth only for finite time.
The validity of such counterexample, however, still requires careful examination.
(The editor is not an expert in this. Please do some fact check yourself.)</p>
</aside>
