</introduction>
<section xml:id="sec-measure">
<title>Lebesgue Measures in <m>\R^n</m></title>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-0910">
<title>20260910</title>
<p>Consider the <m>n</m>-dimensional interval
<md>I = \set{x=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\in\R^n \mid a_j\le x_j\le b_j, j=1,\ldots,n}.</md>
It is reasonable to define the volume of this set to be
<md>\vol(I) \coloneqq \prod_{j=1}^{n}(b_j-a_j).</md>
Let <m>E</m> be any subset of <m>\R^n</m>, and let <m>S=\set{I_1,I_2,\ldots}</m> be a countable collection of intervals.
We call <m>S</m> an <em>interval cover</em> of <m>E</m> if <m>E\subset\bigcup_{j}I_j</m>.
Moreover, define
<md>\sigma(S) \coloneqq \sum_{I_j\in S}\vol(I_j).</md></p>
<definition xml:id="def-outer-measure">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E</m> be any subset of <m>\R^n</m>.
The <em>(Lebesgue) outer measure</em> of <m>E</m> is defined to be
<md>\abs{E}_e \coloneqq \inf \sigma(S),</md>
where the infimum is taken over all interval covers of <m>E</m>.
By abuse of notation, we will write <m>\abs{E}_e = \inf_{S\supset E}\sigma(S)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<question xml:id="q-other-bases">
<p>Would the theory be different if we don't use intervals as our basic of measurement?</p>
</question>
<theorem xml:id="thm-outer-measure-interval">
<statement>
<p>For an interval <m>I</m>, <m>\abs{I}_e=\vol(I)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Since <m>\set{I}</m> covers <m>I</m>, <m>\abs{I}_e\le\vol(I)</m>.
Conversely, let <m>S=\set{I_j}</m> cover <m>I</m> and let <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
Enlarge each <m>I_j</m> to an interval <m>\widetilde I_j</m> with <m>I_j\subset\mathrm{int}(\widetilde I_j)</m> and <m>\vol(\widetilde I_j)\le(1+\eps)\vol(I_j)</m>.
By compactness, <m>I\subset\mathrm{int}(\widetilde I_{j_1})\cup\cdots\cup\mathrm{int}(\widetilde I_{j_N})</m>, so
<md>\vol(I)\le\sum_{i=1}^{N}\vol(\widetilde I_{j_i})\le(1+\eps)\sigma(S).</md>
Letting <m>\eps\to0</m> and taking the infimum over <m>S</m> gives <m>\vol(I)\le\abs{I}_e</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-outer-monotone">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E_1</m>, <m>E_2</m> be subsets of <m>\R^n</m>.
If <m>E_1\subset E_2</m>, then <m>\abs{E_1}_e\le\abs{E_2}_e</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Every interval cover of <m>E_2</m> is an interval cover of <m>E_1</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-outer-subadditive">
<title>Subadditivity of Outer Measures</title>
<statement>
<p>If
<md>E = \bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} E_{k},</md>
then
<md>\abs{E}_e \le \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} \abs{E_k}_e.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>We may assume that <m>\abs{E_k}_e&lt;+\infty</m> for all <m>k</m>.
Let <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, and for each <m>k</m> choose an interval cover <m>S_k</m> of <m>E_k</m> with <m>\sigma(S_k)&lt;\abs{E_k}_e+\eps/2^k</m>.
Then <m>S=\bigcup_k S_k</m> is a countable interval cover of <m>E</m>, so
<md>\abs{E}_e\le\sigma(S)\le\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\sigma(S_k)&lt;\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\abs{E_k}_e+\eps.</md>
Let <m>\eps\to0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<lemma xml:id="lem-outer-regular">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E\subset\R^n</m> and <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
Then there is an open set <m>G\supset E</m> with <m>\abs{G}_e\le\abs{E}_e+\eps</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>We may assume <m>\abs{E}_e&lt;+\infty</m>.
Choose an interval cover <m>\set{I_k}</m> of <m>E</m> with <m>\sum_k\vol(I_k)&lt;\abs{E}_e+\eps/2</m>, and intervals <m>J_k</m> with <m>I_k\subset\mathrm{int}(J_k)</m> and <m>\vol(J_k)&lt;\vol(I_k)+\eps/2^{k+1}</m>.
Then <m>G=\bigcup_k\mathrm{int}(J_k)</m> is open, contains <m>E</m>, and <m>\abs{G}_e\le\sum_k\vol(J_k)&lt;\abs{E}_e+\eps</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<corollary xml:id="cor-countable-null">
<statement>
<p>Any countable subset of <m>\R^n</m> has outer measure zero.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>E=\set{p_1,p_2,\ldots}</m> and <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
Cover <m>p_j</m> by an interval <m>I_j</m> with <m>\vol(I_j)&lt;\eps/2^j</m>; then <m>\abs{E}_e\le\sum_j\vol(I_j)&lt;\eps</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<definition xml:id="def-measurable-set">
<statement>
<p>A subset <m>E</m> of <m>\R^n</m> is <em>(Lebesgue) measurable</em> if for every <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, there exists an open set <m>G</m> containing <m>E</m> such that <m>\abs{G\setminus E}_e&lt;\eps</m>.
In this case, we define <m>\abs{E}\coloneqq \abs{E}_e</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<corollary xml:id="cor-open-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Every open set is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Take <m>G=E</m> in Definition <xref ref="def-measurable-set" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<p>As we will see later, open sets being measurable is an ideal property.
A natural question to ask is the following:</p>
<question xml:id="q-closed-measurable">
<p>Are closed sets measurable? Yes, and we will work our way to the proof of this (Theorem <xref ref="thm-closed-measurable" text="global"/>).</p>
</question>
<lemma xml:id="lem-null-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Every set of outer measure zero is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>If <m>\abs{E}_e=0</m>, Lemma <xref ref="lem-outer-regular" text="global"/> gives open <m>G\supset E</m> with <m>\abs{G\setminus E}_e\le\abs{G}_e&lt;\eps</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-monotone" text="global"/>).</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<remark xml:id="rmk-riemann-volume">
<p>Let <m>U</m> be a bounded open subset of <m>\R^n</m>.
Then <m>U</m> has Riemann volume if and only if <m>\pr U</m> has measure zero.
Recall that if
<md>
<mrow>1_U(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            1 &amp; \text{if } x\in U \\
            0 &amp; \text{if } x\notin U
        \end{cases},</mrow>
</md>
then <m>U</m> has Riemann volume if and only if <m>1_U(x)</m> is Riemann integrable.
Recall that <m>\vol(U)=\int 1_U(x)\,dx</m>.
Then a natural question arises: is <m>\vol(U)=\abs{U}=\abs{U}_e</m>?</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-countable-union">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E_k</m> be a measurable set for <m>k=1,2,\ldots</m>.
Then
<md>E \coloneqq \bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} E_k</md>
is measurable and
<md>\abs{E} \le \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\abs{E_k}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Given <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, choose open <m>G_k\supset E_k</m> with <m>\abs{G_k\setminus E_k}_e&lt;\eps/2^k</m>.
Then <m>G=\bigcup_kG_k</m> is open, contains <m>E</m>, and <m>G\setminus E\subset\bigcup_k(G_k\setminus E_k)</m>, so <m>\abs{G\setminus E}_e&lt;\eps</m> by Theorems <xref ref="thm-outer-monotone" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>.
The inequality is Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>For an interval <m>I\subset \R^n</m>, we denote its boundary by <m>\pr I</m>.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-boundary-null">
<statement>
<p><m>\pr I</m> has measure zero.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>I=\prod_{j}[a_j,b_j]</m> and <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
The face <m>\set{x\in I\mid x_j=a_j}</m> lies in the interval obtained from <m>I</m> by replacing <m>[a_j,b_j]</m> with <m>[a_j-\eps,a_j+\eps]</m>, whose volume is <m>2\eps\prod_{k\ne j}(b_k-a_k)</m>; similarly for <m>x_j=b_j</m>.
The <m>2n</m> faces cover <m>\pr I</m>, so <m>\abs{\pr I}_e\le C\eps</m> with <m>C</m> independent of <m>\eps</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-interval-measurable">
<statement>
<p>An interval <m>I</m> is measurable and <m>\abs{I}=\vol(I)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>I=\mathrm{int}(I)\cup\pr I</m> is measurable by Corollary <xref ref="cor-open-measurable" text="global"/>, Lemmas <xref ref="lem-boundary-null" text="global"/> and <xref ref="lem-null-measurable" text="global"/>, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-countable-union" text="global"/>.
Moreover, <m>\abs{I}=\vol(I)</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-measure-interval" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-nonoverlapping">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>I_1</m>, <m>I_2</m> be intervals.
We say that <m>I_1</m> and <m>I_2</m> are <em>nonoverlapping</em> if
<md>I_1\cap I_2 \subset \pr I_1 \cup \pr I_2.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<theorem xml:id="thm-open-decomposition">
<statement>
<p>Every open set in <m>\R^n</m> can be written as a countable union of nonoverlapping intervals.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>O</m> be open.
For <m>k\in\N_0</m> and <m>m\in\Z^n</m>, let <m>Q_{k,m}=\prod_{j=1}^{n}\left[\frac{m_j}{2^k},\frac{m_j+1}{2^k}\right]</m> be the closed dyadic cubes of side at most <m>1</m>; there are countably many.
Two such cubes are either nonoverlapping or one contains the other.
Let <m>\mathcal Q</m> be the set of dyadic cubes <m>Q\subset O</m> that are maximal with respect to inclusion; maximal cubes exist because the cubes containing a given one form a finite chain.
Distinct members of <m>\mathcal Q</m> are nonoverlapping.
If <m>x\in O</m>, then <m>B(x,\eps)\subset O</m> for some <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, and a dyadic cube containing <m>x</m> of side <m>&lt;\eps/\sqrt n</m> lies in <m>O</m>, hence in a member of <m>\mathcal Q</m>.
Thus <m>O=\bigcup_{Q\in\mathcal Q}Q</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-0915">
<title>20260915</title>
<p>Today is Serre's 100th birthday.</p>
<definition xml:id="def-set-distance">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E_1</m> and <m>E_2</m> be two sets in <m>\R^n</m>.
Define
<md>d(E_1,E_2)\coloneqq \inf\set{|x_1-x_2| \mid x_1\in E_1, x_2\in E_2}.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<lemma xml:id="lem-separated-additive">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E_1</m> and <m>E_2</m> be two sets in <m>\R^n</m>.
If <m>d(E_1,E_2)&gt;0</m>, then <m>\abs{E_1\cup E_2}_e = \abs{E_1}_e + \abs{E_2}_e</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>, it suffices to show <m>\ge</m>.
Let <m>\eps&gt;0</m> and let <m>S</m> be an interval cover of <m>E_1\cup E_2</m> with <m>\sigma(S)&lt;\abs{E_1\cup E_2}_e+\eps</m>; subdividing, we may assume every <m>I\in S</m> has diameter <m>&lt;d(E_1,E_2)</m>.
Then no <m>I\in S</m> meets both <m>E_1</m> and <m>E_2</m>, so <m>S</m> splits into disjoint covers <m>S_1</m> of <m>E_1</m> and <m>S_2</m> of <m>E_2</m>, and
<md>\abs{E_1}_e+\abs{E_2}_e\le\sigma(S_1)+\sigma(S_2)\le\sigma(S)&lt;\abs{E_1\cup E_2}_e+\eps.</md></p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-finite-nonoverlapping">
<statement>
<p>If <m>\set{I_k}_{k=1}^{N}</m> is a finite collection of nonoverlapping intervals, then
<md>\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{N} I_k \right\rvert = \sum_{k=1}^{N} \abs{I_k}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>, it suffices to show <m>\ge</m>.
For small <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, let <m>I_{k,\eps}\subset\mathrm{int}(I_k)</m> be the interval obtained by shrinking each side of <m>I_k</m> by <m>\eps</m> at both ends.
These are disjoint compact sets, so they have pairwise positive distance, and Lemma <xref ref="lem-separated-additive" text="global"/> (applied <m>N-1</m> times) gives
<md>\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{N}I_k \right\rvert\ge\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{N}I_{k,\eps} \right\rvert=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\abs{I_{k,\eps}}.</md>
Let <m>\eps\to0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-closed-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Every closed set is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>First let <m>F</m> be compact and <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-outer-regular" text="global"/>, there is open <m>G\supset F</m> with <m>\abs{G}\le\abs{F}_e+\eps</m>.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-open-decomposition" text="global"/>, the open set <m>G\setminus F</m> is a union of nonoverlapping intervals <m>J_1,J_2,\ldots</m>.
For each <m>N</m>, the compact set <m>K_N=\bigcup_{k\le N}J_k</m> is disjoint from <m>F</m>, so <m>d(F,K_N)&gt;0</m>, and Lemmas <xref ref="lem-separated-additive" text="global"/> and <xref ref="lem-finite-nonoverlapping" text="global"/> give
<md>\abs{F}_e+\eps\ge\abs{G}\ge\abs{F\cup K_N}_e=\abs{F}_e+\sum_{k=1}^{N}\abs{J_k}.</md>
Hence <m>\abs{G\setminus F}_e\le\sum_k\abs{J_k}\le\eps</m>, so <m>F</m> is measurable.
For a general closed <m>F</m>, write <m>F=\bigcup_k(F\cap\set{\abs x\le k})</m> and apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-countable-union" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-complement-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E\subset\R^n</m> be a measurable set.
Then <m>E^c=\R^n\setminus E</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Choose open <m>G_k\supset E</m> with <m>\abs{G_k\setminus E}_e&lt;1/k</m>.
Then <m>H=\bigcup_kG_k^c\subset E^c</m> is measurable by Theorems <xref ref="thm-closed-measurable" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-countable-union" text="global"/>.
Since <m>Z=E^c\setminus H\subset G_k\setminus E</m> for every <m>k</m>, <m>\abs{Z}_e=0</m>, so <m>Z</m> is measurable by Lemma <xref ref="lem-null-measurable" text="global"/>.
Hence <m>E^c=H\cup Z</m> is measurable.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-countable-intersection">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>\set{E_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> be measurable sets.
Then
<md>E = \bigcap_{k=1}^{+\infty} E_k</md>
is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>E=\left(\bigcup_kE_k^c\right)^c</m>; apply Theorems <xref ref="thm-complement-measurable" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-countable-union" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-difference-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E_1</m> and <m>E_2</m> be measurable sets.
Then <m>E_1\setminus E_2</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>E_1\setminus E_2=E_1\cap E_2^c</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-sigma-algebra">
<statement>
<p>Let
<md>\Sigma = \set{S_j\subset \R^n \mid j\in J},</md>
where <m>J</m> is an index set.
We say that <m>\Sigma</m> is a <em><m>\sigma</m>-algebra</em> if the following holds:
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>E^c\in\Sigma</m> if <m>E\in\Sigma</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If <m>E_k\in\Sigma</m> for all <m>k</m>, then
<md>\bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} E_k \in \Sigma.</md></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>Theorems <xref ref="thm-countable-union" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-complement-measurable" text="global"/> can be summarized as</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-measurable-sigma-algebra">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>M</m> be the set of all measurable subsets of <m>\R^n</m>.
Then <m>M</m> is a <m>\sigma</m>-algebra.</p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-limsup-sets">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>\set{A_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> be subsets of <m>\R^n</m>.
Define
<md>\limsup_{k\to+\infty} A_k \coloneqq \bigcap_{j=1}^{+\infty}\bigcup_{k=j}^{+\infty}A_k, \quad
        \liminf_{k\to+\infty} A_k \coloneqq \bigcup_{j=1}^{+\infty}\bigcap_{k=j}^{+\infty}A_k.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<aside>
<title>Supplement</title>
<p>Notice that for any sequence <m>x_k</m> of real numbers, we have
<md>\limsup_{k\to +\infty} \,(-\infty, x_k) = \left(-\infty,\limsup_{k\to+\infty}x_k\right), \quad
        \liminf_{k\to +\infty} \,(-\infty, x_k) = \left(-\infty,\liminf_{k\to+\infty}x_k\right).</md>
In view of this, we may regard every real number <m>x</m> as the subset <m>(-\infty, x)</m> of <m>\R</m> and this explains the notations.</p>
</aside>
<corollary xml:id="cor-limsup-measurable">
<statement>
<p>If <m>\set{E_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> is a set of measurable sets, then <m>\limsup_{k\to+\infty} E_k</m> and <m>\liminf_{k\to+\infty} E_k</m> are measurable.</p>
</statement>
</corollary>
<definition xml:id="def-gdelta-fsigma">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>H\subset\R^n</m> be a subset.
We say that <m>H</m> is a <em><m>G_\delta</m> set</em> if
<md>H = \bigcap_{k=1}^{+\infty} G_k,</md>
where <m>G_k</m> are open sets.
We say that <m>H</m> is a <em><m>F_\sigma</m> set</em> if
<md>H = \bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} F_k,</md>
where <m>F_k</m> are closed sets.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<aside>
<title>Supplement</title>
<p>The notation <m>G_\delta</m> originated from the German words <em>Gebiet</em> (open set) and <em>Durchschnitt</em> (intersection); amusingly, <m>F_\sigma</m> orginated from the French words <em>fermé</em> (closed) and <em>somme</em> (union).
Nouns in German are capitalized.</p>
</aside>
<corollary xml:id="cor-gdelta-fsigma-measurable">
<statement>
<p>All <m>G_\delta</m> sets and <m>F_\sigma</m> sets are measurable.</p>
</statement>
</corollary>
<remark xml:id="rmk-open-not-sigma">
<p>Let <m>\widetilde B</m> be the set of all open sets of <m>\R^n</m>.
Then <m>\widetilde B</m> is not a <m>\sigma</m>-algebra.</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-borel-exists">
<statement>
<p>There is a unique smallest <m>\sigma</m>-algebra <m>B</m> containing <m>\widetilde B</m>.
In other words, for every <m>\sigma</m>-algebra <m>B'</m> with <m>B'\supset\widetilde B</m>, one has <m>B'\supset B</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>The power set <m>\mathcal P(\R^n)</m> is a <m>\sigma</m>-algebra containing <m>\widetilde B</m>, and an intersection of <m>\sigma</m>-algebras is a <m>\sigma</m>-algebra.
So <m>B</m> is the intersection of all <m>\sigma</m>-algebras containing <m>\widetilde B</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-borel">
<statement>
<p><m>B</m> is called the <em>Borel <m>\sigma</m>-algebra</em>.
The sets in <m>B</m> are called <em>Borel sets</em> of <m>\R^n</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<corollary xml:id="cor-gdelta-borel">
<statement>
<p>All <m>G_\delta</m> and <m>F_\sigma</m> are Borel sets.</p>
</statement>
</corollary>
<theorem xml:id="thm-borel-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Every Borel set is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>The measurable sets form a <m>\sigma</m>-algebra containing <m>\widetilde B</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-measurable-sigma-algebra" text="global"/> and Corollary <xref ref="cor-open-measurable" text="global"/>), so they contain <m>B</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-borel-exists" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<question xml:id="q-non-borel">
<p>Is there a measurable set which is not a Borel set?
The answer is positive, and in fact, there are many more measurable sets then Borel sets.</p>
</question>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-0917">
<title>20260917</title>
<lemma xml:id="lem-inner-closed">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E</m> be any subset of <m>\R^n</m>.
Then <m>E</m> is measurable if and only if for every <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, there exists a closed set <m>F\subset E</m> such that <m>\abs{E\setminus F}_e&lt;\eps</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Closed sets <m>F\subset E</m> correspond to open sets <m>G=F^c\supset E^c</m>, with <m>E\setminus F=G\setminus E^c</m>.
So the condition says exactly that <m>E^c</m> is measurable, which is equivalent to <m>E</m> being measurable by Theorem <xref ref="thm-complement-measurable" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-additivity">
<title>Additivity of Measures</title>
<statement>
<p>If <m>\set{E_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> is a countable collection of disjoint measurable sets, then
<md>\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} E_k \right\rvert = \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\abs{E_k}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>, it suffices to show <m>\ge</m>.
First assume every <m>E_k</m> is bounded.
Given <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, Lemma <xref ref="lem-inner-closed" text="global"/> gives closed <m>F_k\subset E_k</m> with <m>\abs{E_k\setminus F_k}&lt;\eps/2^k</m>, so <m>\abs{F_k}\ge\abs{E_k}-\eps/2^k</m>.
The <m>F_k</m> are disjoint and compact, hence pairwise at positive distance, and Lemma <xref ref="lem-separated-additive" text="global"/> gives
<md>\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty}E_k \right\rvert\ge\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{N}F_k \right\rvert=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\abs{F_k}\ge\sum_{k=1}^{N}\abs{E_k}-\eps.</md>
Let <m>N\to+\infty</m> and <m>\eps\to0</m>.
In general, put <m>S_j=\set{j-1\le\abs x&lt;j}</m>; the sets <m>E_k\cap S_j</m> are bounded, disjoint and measurable, so by the bounded case
<md>\left\lvert \bigcup_kE_k \right\rvert=\sum_{k,j}\abs{E_k\cap S_j}=\sum_k\sum_j\abs{E_k\cap S_j}=\sum_k\abs{E_k}.</md></p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-nonoverlapping-additive">
<statement>
<p>If <m>\set{I_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> is a collection of nonoverlapping intervals, then
<md>\left\lvert \bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} I_k \right\rvert = \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} \abs{I_k}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>The interiors <m>\mathrm{int}(I_k)</m> are disjoint, and <m>\abs{\mathrm{int}(I_k)}=\abs{I_k}</m> by Lemma <xref ref="lem-boundary-null" text="global"/>.
So Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/> gives <m>\ge</m>, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/> gives <m>\le</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<corollary xml:id="cor-measure-difference">
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>E_1</m> and <m>E_2</m> are measurable sets with <m>E_2\subset E_1</m>.
Then
<md>\abs{E_1\setminus E_2} +  \abs{E_2} = \abs{E_1}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/> to the disjoint union <m>E_1=(E_1\setminus E_2)\cup E_2</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<theorem xml:id="thm-continuity-measure">
<title>Continuity from Below and Above</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>\set{E_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> be a sequence of measurable sets.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>Let <m>E=\bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty}E_k</m>.
Suppose <m>E_1\subset E_2\subset E_3\subset \cdots</m>; this is sometimes denoted by <m>E_k \nearrow E</m>.
Then <m>\abs{E}=\lim_{k\to+\infty}\abs{E_k}</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Let <m>E=\bigcap_{k=1}^{+\infty}E_k</m>.
Suppose <m>E_1\supset E_2\supset E_3\supset \cdots</m> and <m>\abs{E_k}&lt;+\infty</m> for some <m>k</m>; this is sometimes denoted by <m>E_k\searrow E</m>.
Then <m>\abs{E}=\lim_{k\to+\infty}\abs{E_k}</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>(i) We may assume all <m>\abs{E_k}&lt;+\infty</m>.
Write <m>E</m> as the disjoint union <m>E_1\cup\bigcup_{k\ge2}(E_k\setminus E_{k-1})</m>.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/> and Corollary <xref ref="cor-measure-difference" text="global"/> (all measures being finite),
<md>\abs{E}=\abs{E_1}+\sum_{k=2}^{+\infty}(\abs{E_k}-\abs{E_{k-1}})=\lim_{k\to+\infty}\abs{E_k}.</md>
(ii) Discarding finitely many sets, assume <m>\abs{E_1}&lt;+\infty</m>.
Then <m>E_1\setminus E_k\nearrow E_1\setminus E</m>, so (i) and Corollary <xref ref="cor-measure-difference" text="global"/> give <m>\abs{E_1}-\abs{E_k}\to\abs{E_1}-\abs{E}</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-gdelta-fsigma-char">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E</m> be a subset of <m>\R^n</m>.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>E</m> is measurable if and only if <m>E=H\setminus Z</m> for some <m>G_\delta</m> set <m>H</m> and for some <m>Z</m> with measure zero.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>E</m> is measurable if and only if <m>E=H\cup Z</m> for some <m>F_\sigma</m> set <m>H</m> and for some <m>Z</m> with measure zero.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>The <em>if</em> directions follow from Corollary <xref ref="cor-gdelta-fsigma-measurable" text="global"/> and Lemma <xref ref="lem-null-measurable" text="global"/>.
(i) Choose open <m>G_k\supset E</m> with <m>\abs{G_k\setminus E}&lt;1/k</m>, and let <m>H=\bigcap_kG_k</m> and <m>Z=H\setminus E</m>.
Then <m>Z\subset G_k\setminus E</m> for all <m>k</m>, so <m>\abs{Z}=0</m>.
(ii) By Lemma <xref ref="lem-inner-closed" text="global"/>, choose closed <m>F_k\subset E</m> with <m>\abs{E\setminus F_k}&lt;1/k</m>, and let <m>H=\bigcup_kF_k</m> and <m>Z=E\setminus H\subset E\setminus F_k</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-caratheodory">
<title>Carathéodory's Criterion</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E</m> be a subset of <m>\R^n</m>.
Then <m>E</m> is measurable if and only if
<md>\abs{A}_e = \abs{A\cap E}_e + \abs{A\setminus E}_e</md>
for every set <m>A</m> in <m>\R^n</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Lemma <xref ref="lem-outer-regular" text="global"/>, every <m>A\subset\R^n</m> is contained in a <m>G_\delta</m> set <m>H</m> with <m>\abs{H}=\abs{A}_e</m>.
The inequality <m>\le</m> always holds by Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>.</p>
<p>Suppose <m>E</m> is measurable and <m>A\subset H</m> as above.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/>,
<md>\abs{A}_e=\abs{H}=\abs{H\cap E}+\abs{H\setminus E}\ge\abs{A\cap E}_e+\abs{A\setminus E}_e.</md></p>
<p>Conversely, assume the identity, and let <m>E_k=E\cap\set{\abs x\le k}</m>.
Take a <m>G_\delta</m> set <m>H_k\supset E_k</m> with <m>\abs{H_k}=\abs{E_k}_e&lt;+\infty</m>.
Applying the identity with <m>A=H_k</m> and using <m>H_k\cap E\supset E_k</m>,
<md>\abs{H_k}\ge\abs{E_k}_e+\abs{H_k\setminus E}_e,</md>
so <m>\abs{H_k\setminus E}_e=0</m>.
With <m>H=\bigcup_kH_k\supset E</m>, the set <m>H\setminus E=\bigcup_k(H_k\setminus E)</m> has measure zero, and <m>E=H\setminus(H\setminus E)</m> is measurable by Lemma <xref ref="lem-null-measurable" text="global"/> and Theorem <xref ref="thm-difference-measurable" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>As an ending to this chapter, we are going to show that there exists a nonmeasurable set.</p>
<aside>
<title>Supplement</title>
<p>Let <m>\set{A_\alpha}_{\alpha\in I}</m> be an indexed family of nonempty disjoint sets, where <m>I</m> is an index set.
<em>Axiom of Choice</em> (AC) says that there exists a <em>choice function</em>
<md>f \colon I\to \bigcup_{\alpha\in I}A_\alpha</md>
such that <m>f(\alpha)\in A_\alpha</m> for all <m>\alpha\in I</m>.</p>
</aside>
<theorem xml:id="thm-vitali">
<title>Vitali</title>
<statement>
<p>There exists a nonmeasurable set <m>V\subset[0,1]\subset\R</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Write <m>x\sim y</m> if <m>x-y\in\Q</m>.
By the Axiom of Choice, let <m>V\subset[0,1]</m> contain exactly one point of each equivalence class meeting <m>[0,1]</m>.
Enumerate <m>\Q\cap[-1,1]=\set{q_1,q_2,\ldots}</m> and put <m>V_k=V+q_k</m>.
The <m>V_k</m> are disjoint (if <m>v+q_k=v'+q_\ell</m>, then <m>v\sim v'</m>, so <m>v=v'</m> and <m>k=\ell</m>), and <m>[0,1]\subset\bigcup_kV_k\subset[-1,2]</m> (each <m>x\in[0,1]</m> is equivalent to some <m>v\in V</m>, and <m>x-v\in\Q\cap[-1,1]</m>).
If <m>V</m> were measurable, then so is each <m>V_k</m>, with <m>\abs{V_k}=\abs{V}</m>, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/> would give
<md>1\le\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\abs{V}\le3,</md>
which is impossible both when <m>\abs V=0</m> and when <m>\abs V&gt;0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<remark xml:id="rmk-solovay">
<p>In 1970, Solovay proved that it is not possible to construct nonmeasurable sets without Axiom of Choice.
In other words, all sets definable with explicit formulas or algorithms (in a certain framework) must be measurable.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>
</section>
<section xml:id="sec-functions">
<title>Lebesgue Measurable Functions</title>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-0922">
<title>20260922</title>
<p>Let <m>\overline\R\coloneqq \R\cup\set{-\infty,+\infty}</m>.</p>
<definition xml:id="def-measurable-function">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset\R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
We say that <m>f</m> is a <em>(Lebesgue) measurable function</em> on <m>E</m> if for every <m>a\in\R</m>, the set <m>\set{x\in E\mid f(x)&gt;a}</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<theorem xml:id="thm-level-sets">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset\R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
Assume that <m>E</m> is measurable.
Then the following are equivalent:
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>\set{x\in E\mid f(x)&gt; a}</m> is measurable for all <m>a\in \R</m> (and thus <m>f</m> is measurable by definition);</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\set{x\in E\mid f(x)\ge a}</m> is measurable for all <m>a\in\R</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\set{x\in E\mid f(x)&lt; a}</m> is measurable for all <m>a\in\R</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\set{x\in E\mid f(x)\le a}</m> is measurable for all <m>a\in\R</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-measurable-sigma-algebra" text="global"/>, this follows from
<md>\set{f\ge a}=\bigcap_{k}\set{f&gt;a-1/k},\quad
        \set{f&lt;a}=E\setminus\set{f\ge a},\quad
        \set{f\le a}=\bigcap_{k}\set{f&lt;a+1/k},\quad
        \set{f&gt;a}=E\setminus\set{f\le a}.</md></p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<remark xml:id="rmk-function-conventions">
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset\R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
From now on, we assume that <m>E</m> is measurable.
For each <m>S\subset\overline\R</m>, let <m>f^{-1}(S)=\set{x\in E\mid f(x)\in S}</m>.
The set <m>\set{f&gt; a}</m> will be understood to be <m>\set{x\in E\mid f(x)&gt; a}</m> if the domain is not specified; the same convention applies to other variants.</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-open-preimage">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
Then <m>f</m> is measurable if and only if for every open set <m>G\subset\R</m>, the set <m>f^{-1}(G)</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><em>If</em>: <m>\set{f&gt;a}=f^{-1}((a,+\infty))</m>, where for extended-valued <m>f</m> the interval <m>(a,+\infty)</m> is understood as <m>(a,+\infty]</m>.
<em>Only if</em>: for <m>I=[a,b]</m>, <m>f^{-1}(I)=\set{f\le b}\setminus\set{f&lt;a}</m> is measurable by Theorem <xref ref="thm-level-sets" text="global"/>, and every open <m>G</m> is a countable union of intervals <m>I_k</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-open-decomposition" text="global"/>), so <m>f^{-1}(G)=\bigcup_kf^{-1}(I_k)</m> is measurable.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-continuous-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\R</m> be a continuous function, where <m>E</m> is an open set.
Then <m>f</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>For open <m>G</m>, <m>f^{-1}(G)</m> is open, hence measurable; apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-open-preimage" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<definition xml:id="def-ae">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be two functions.
We say that <m>f=g</m> <em>almost everywhere</em> (abbreviated as a.e.) if there exists a subset <m>Z</m> of <m>E</m> with measure zero such that <m>f=g</m> on <m>E\setminus Z</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<lemma xml:id="lem-ae-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be two functions.
If <m>f</m> is measurable and <m>f=g</m> a.e., then <m>g</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>Z\supset\set{f\ne g}</m> have measure zero.
Then <m>\set{g&gt;a}=(\set{f&gt;a}\setminus Z)\cup(\set{g&gt;a}\cap Z)</m>, and both pieces are measurable (the second by Lemma <xref ref="lem-null-measurable" text="global"/>).</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-composition">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
Let <m>\phi:\R\to\R</m> be a continuous function.
Assume that <m>f</m> is finite a.e.; in other words, there exists a measure zero set <m>Z</m> contained in <m>E</m> such that <m>f(x)\notin\set{-\infty,+\infty}</m> for all <m>x\in E\setminus Z</m>.
Then <m>\phi(f)\colon E\to\overline\R</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>For open <m>G\subset\R</m>, <m>\phi^{-1}(G)</m> is open, so <m>(\phi\circ f)^{-1}(G)=f^{-1}(\phi^{-1}(G))</m> is measurable up to a subset of <m>Z</m>.
Conclude by Theorem <xref ref="thm-open-preimage" text="global"/> and Lemma <xref ref="lem-ae-measurable" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-abs-pow-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
Then <m>\abs{f}^p</m> (<m>p&gt;0</m>), <m>e^f</m>, <m>f^+\coloneqq\max\set{f,0}</m>, and <m>f^-\coloneqq-\min\set{f,0}</m> are measurable.</p>
</statement>
</corollary>
<theorem xml:id="thm-sum-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
Then <m>f+g</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Since <m>f&gt;a-g</m> holds exactly when some rational <m>q</m> lies strictly between,
<md>\set{f+g&gt;a}=\bigcup_{q\in\Q}\left(\set{f&gt;q}\cap\set{g&gt;a-q}\right).</md></p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-product-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
Then <m>fg</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>fg=\frac14\left((f+g)^2-(f-g)^2\right)</m>; apply Theorems <xref ref="thm-sum-measurable" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-composition" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-sup-inf-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function for each <m>k\in\N</m>.
Then <m>\sup_kf_k(x)</m> and <m>\inf_kf_k(x)</m> are measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>\set{\sup_kf_k&gt;a}=\bigcup_k\set{f_k&gt;a}</m> and <m>\set{\inf_kf_k&lt;a}=\bigcup_k\set{f_k&lt;a}</m>; use Theorem <xref ref="thm-level-sets" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-limsup-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function for each <m>k\in\N</m>.
Then <m>\limsup_{k\to+\infty}f_k(x)</m> and <m>\liminf_{k\to+\infty}f_k(x)</m> are measurable.
(Recall that <m>\limsup_{k\to+\infty}a_k</m> (resp. <m>\liminf_{k\to+\infty}a_k</m>) is the supremum (resp. infimum) of all limits of convergent subsequences of <m>a_k</m>.)</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>\limsup_kf_k=\inf_k\sup_{j\ge k}f_j</m> and <m>\liminf_kf_k=\sup_k\inf_{j\ge k}f_j</m>; apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-sup-inf-measurable" text="global"/> twice.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-0924">
<title>20260924</title>
<definition xml:id="def-characteristic">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E</m> be a subset of <m>\R^n</m>.
The <em>characteristic function</em> of <m>E</m> is defined by
<md>
<mrow>1_E(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            1 &amp; \text{if } x\in E \\
            0 &amp; \text{if } x\notin E
       \end{cases}.</mrow>
</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<definition xml:id="def-simple">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon\R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
We say that <m>f</m> is a <em>simple function</em> if
<md>f(x) = \sum_{k=1}^{N}a_k 1_{E_k}(x),</md>
where <m>a_k\in\R</m>, <m>a_k\ne a_\ell</m> if <m>k\ne \ell</m>, and <m>E_1,\ldots,E_N</m> are disjoint subsets of <m>\R^n</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<lemma xml:id="lem-simple-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let
<md>f = \sum_{k=1}^{N}a_k1_{E_k}</md>
be a simple function.
Then <m>f</m> is measurable if and only if <m>E_k</m> is measurable for every <m>k</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>If <m>f</m> is measurable, then <m>E_k=\set{a_k-\eps&lt;f&lt;a_k+\eps}</m> for <m>\eps&gt;0</m> smaller than half the gap between the distinct values <m>a_j</m>, which is measurable.
Conversely, <m>\set{f&gt;a}</m> is the union of those <m>E_k</m> with <m>a_k&gt;a</m>, together with the complement of <m>\bigcup_kE_k</m> (where <m>f=0</m>) if <m>a&lt;0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-simple-approximation">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
Then there is a sequence <m>\set{f_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> of measurable simple functions such that for all <m>x\in E</m>,
<md>\lim_{k\to+\infty} f_k(x) = f(x).</md>
Moreover, if <m>f\ge 0</m>, then <m>\set{f_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> can be chosen to be increasing, i.e., <m>f_1\le f_2\le \cdots</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>f\ge0</m> first, and put
<md>f_k=\sum_{j=1}^{k2^k}\frac{j-1}{2^k}1_{\set{(j-1)/2^k\le f&lt;j/2^k}}+k1_{\set{f\ge k}},</md>
which is a measurable simple function by Lemma <xref ref="lem-simple-measurable" text="global"/>.
If <m>f(x)&lt;+\infty</m>, then <m>0\le f(x)-f_k(x)\le2^{-k}</m> once <m>k&gt;f(x)</m>; if <m>f(x)=+\infty</m>, then <m>f_k(x)=k\to+\infty</m>.
Since each dyadic interval of level <m>k</m> splits into two of level <m>k+1</m>, and <m>k1_{\set{f\ge k}}</m> increases with <m>k</m>, we have <m>f_k\le f_{k+1}</m>.
For general <m>f</m>, apply this to <m>f^+</m> and <m>f^-</m> (Corollary <xref ref="cor-abs-pow-measurable" text="global"/>) and subtract.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-semicontinuous">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
Assume that <m>x_0\in E</m> is a limit point of <m>E</m>.
We say that <m>f</m> is <em>upper semicontinuous</em> at <m>x_0</m> if
<md>\limsup_{x\to x_0,\, x\ne x_0} f(x) \le f(x_0).</md>
We say that <m>f</m> is <em>upper semicontinous</em> on <m>E</m> if <m>f</m> is upper semicontinuous at every limit points <m>x\in E</m>.</p>
<p>Likewise, we say that <m>f</m> is <em>lower semicontinuous</em> at <m>x_0</m> if
<md>\liminf_{x\to x_0,\, x\ne x_0} f(x) \ge f(x_0).</md>
We say that <m>f</m> is <em>lower semicontinous</em> on <m>E</m> if <m>f</m> is lower semicontinuous at every limit points <m>x\in E</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-limsup-formula">
<p>Recall that
<md>
<mrow>&amp; \limsup_{x\to x_0,\, x\ne x_0} f(x)</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= \sup\set{a\in \overline\R \mid \exists \set{x_j}_{j=1}^{+\infty}\subset E,\, x_j\ne x_0\, \forall j\in\N,\, \lim_{j\to+\infty}x_j = x_0,\, \lim_{j\to+\infty} f(x_j)=a}.</mrow>
</md></p>
</remark>
<example xml:id="ex-usc-step">
<p>Consider the function
<md>
<mrow>u(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            0 &amp; \text{if } x&lt;x_0 \\
            1 &amp; \text{if } x\ge x_0
        \end{cases}.</mrow>
</md>
Then
<md>\limsup_{x\to x_0,\, x\ne x_0} u(x) = u(x_0).</md></p>
</example>
<example xml:id="ex-usc-sin">
<p>Consider the function
<md>
<mrow>u(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            \sin \frac{1}{x} &amp; \text{if } x&gt;0 \\
            2 &amp; \text{if } x=0
        \end{cases}.</mrow>
</md>
Then
<md>\limsup_{x\to 0,\, x\ne 0} u(x) = 1 &lt; u(0).</md></p>
</example>
<example xml:id="ex-subharmonic">
<p>Identify <m>\R^2</m> with <m>\C</m>.
For <m>u\in C^{\infty}(\R^2)</m>, <m>u</m> is <em>harmonic</em> if <m>\Delta u=0</m>, where <m>\Delta=\pr^2/\pr x^2 + \pr^2/\pr y^2</m>.
We say that <m>u</m> is <em>subharmonic</em> if <m>u</m> is upper semicontinuous and <m>\Delta u\ge 0</m> in the distributional sense.
The latter condition roughly amounts to saying that
<md>\int u\Delta g\ge 0</md>
for all <m>g\ge 0</m>, <m>g\in C_c^{\infty}(\R^2)</m>.
Subharmonic functions play an important role in geometry, potential theory, and various fields of physics.</p>
</example>
<definition xml:id="def-relatively-closed">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>A\subset E</m> be any set.
We say that <m>A</m> is <em>relatively closed in</em> <m>E</m> if there exists a closed set <m>F\subset\R^n</m> such that <m>A=F\cap E</m>.
Equivalently, if <m>\set{x_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> is any sequence in <m>A</m> with <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty} x_k=x_0\in E</m>, then <m>x_0\in A</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<theorem xml:id="thm-usc-characterization">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
Then <m>f</m> is upper semicontinuous if and only if <m>\set{f\ge a}</m> is relatively closed in <m>E</m> for all <m>a\in\R</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Suppose <m>f</m> is upper semicontinuous, and let <m>x_k\in\set{f\ge a}</m> with <m>x_k\to x_0\in E</m>, <m>x_k\ne x_0</m>.
Then <m>f(x_0)\ge\limsup_kf(x_k)\ge a</m>, so <m>x_0\in\set{f\ge a}</m>.</p>
<p>Conversely, suppose <m>f(x_0)&lt;\limsup_{x\to x_0}f(x)</m> for some <m>x_0</m>.
Pick <m>a\in\R</m> with <m>f(x_0)&lt;a&lt;\limsup_{x\to x_0}f(x)</m> and a sequence <m>x_j\to x_0</m>, <m>x_j\ne x_0</m>, with <m>f(x_j)\ge a</m> for all <m>j</m>.
Then the <m>x_j</m> lie in <m>\set{f\ge a}</m> but <m>x_0</m> does not, so <m>\set{f\ge a}</m> is not relatively closed.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>Symmetrically, we have</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-lsc-characterization">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a function.
Then <m>f</m> is lower semicontinuous if and only if <m>\set{f\le a}</m> is relatively closed in <m>E</m> for all <m>a\in\R</m>.</p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-semicontinuous-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to \overline\R</m> be an upper or lower semicontinuous function.
Then <m>f</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
</corollary>
<theorem xml:id="thm-egorov">
<title>Egorov</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\R</m> be a measurable function for each <m>k\in\N</m>.
Suppose that <m>\abs{E}&lt;+\infty</m> and <m>f_k\to f</m> pointwise a.e. on <m>E</m>.
Then for every <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, there exists a closed set <m>F\subset E</m> such that <m>\abs{E\setminus F}&lt;\eps</m> and <m>f_k\to f</m> uniformly on <m>F</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>The limit <m>f</m> is measurable by Theorem <xref ref="thm-limsup-measurable" text="global"/> and Lemma <xref ref="lem-ae-measurable" text="global"/>.
For <m>\ell,m\in\N</m>, let
<md>E_{\ell,m}=\bigcap_{k\ge m}\set{\abs{f_k-f}&lt;1/\ell}.</md>
For fixed <m>\ell</m>, <m>E_{\ell,m}\nearrow E\setminus Z</m> with <m>\abs Z=0</m>, so by Theorem <xref ref="thm-continuity-measure" text="global"/> (and <m>\abs E&lt;+\infty</m>) there is <m>m_\ell</m> with <m>\abs{E\setminus E_{\ell,m_\ell}}&lt;\eps/2^{\ell+1}</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-inner-closed" text="global"/>, choose a closed <m>F_\ell\subset E_{\ell,m_\ell}</m> with <m>\abs{E\setminus F_\ell}&lt;\eps/2^\ell</m>, and let <m>F=\bigcap_\ell F_\ell</m>.
Then <m>F</m> is closed, <m>\abs{E\setminus F}\le\sum_\ell\abs{E\setminus F_\ell}&lt;\eps</m>, and <m>\abs{f_k-f}&lt;1/\ell</m> on <m>F</m> for all <m>k\ge m_\ell</m>; that is, <m>f_k\to f</m> uniformly on <m>F</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-0929">
<title>20260929</title>
<p>Some applications of Egorov's theorem:</p>
<definition xml:id="def-normed-space">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>X</m> be a vector space over <m>\R</m>.
It is called a <em>normed vector space</em> if for every <m>x\in X</m>, there exists a real number called the <em>norm</em> of <m>x</m>, denoted by <m>\norm{x}</m>, satisfying the following:
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>\norm{x}\ge 0</m> for all <m>x\in X</m>, and <m>\norm{x}=0</m> if and only if <m>x=0</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\norm{x+y}\le \norm{x}+\norm{y}</m> for all <m>x,y\in X</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\norm{\alpha x}=\abs{\alpha}\norm{x}</m> for all <m>\alpha\in\R</m>, <m>x\in X</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<example xml:id="ex-euclidean-norm">
<p>Let <m>X=\R^n</m>.
Then <m>X</m> equipped with
<md>\norm{x} = \sqrt{x_1^2+\dots+x_n^2}</md>
is a normed vector space.</p>
</example>
<definition xml:id="def-quasi-normed-space">
<statement>
<p>A vector space <m>X</m> is called a <em>quasi-normed vector space</em> if for every <m>x\in X</m>, there exists a real number called the quasi-norm of <m>x</m>, denoted by <m>\norm{x}</m>, satisfying the following:
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>\norm{x}\ge 0</m> for all <m>x\in X</m>, and <m>\norm{x}=0</m> if and only if <m>x=0</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\norm{x+y}\le \norm{x}+\norm{y}</m> for all <m>x,y\in X</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\norm{x}=\norm{-x}</m> for all <m>x\in X</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>for any <m>x\in X</m> and for any sequence <m>\alpha_n</m> of scalars with <m>\alpha_n\to 0</m>,
<md>\lim_{n\to+\infty} \norm{\alpha_n x} = 0;</md></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>for any scalar <m>\alpha</m> and for any sequence <m>x_n\in X</m> with <m>\norm{x_n}\to 0</m>,
<md>\lim_{n\to+\infty} \norm{\alpha x_n} = 0.</md></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<example xml:id="ex-quasi-norm">
<p>Let <m>X=\R^n</m>.
Then <m>X</m> equipped with
<md>\norm{x} = \frac{\sqrt{x_1^2+\dots+x_n^2}}{1+\sqrt{x_1^2+\dots+x_n^2}}</md>
is a quasi-normed vector space.</p>
</example>
<remark xml:id="rmk-normed-continuity">
<p>In a normed vector space <m>(X,\norm{\cdot})</m>, it is easy to see that <m>\lim_{n\to+\infty}\norm{\alpha_nx_n - \alpha x}=0</m> if <m>\lim_{n\to+\infty}\alpha_n = \alpha</m> and <m>\lim_{n\to+\infty}x_n = x</m>:
<md>
<mrow>&amp; \norm{\alpha_n x_n - \alpha x}</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= \norm{\alpha_n(x_n-x) + (\alpha_n-\alpha)x}</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;\le \norm{\alpha_n(x_n-x)} + \norm{(\alpha_n-\alpha)x}</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= \abs{\alpha_n}\norm{x_n-x} + \abs{\alpha_n-\alpha}\norm{x} \to 0.</mrow>
</md>
We would hope that the same is true for quasi-normed vector spaces.
However, the argument above does not work because <m>\norm{\alpha_n(x_n-x)}</m> is not necessarily equal to <m>\abs{\alpha_n}\norm{x_n-x}</m>.
Fortunately, the same conclusion holds in quasi-normed vector spaces, as we will show in Theorem <xref ref="thm-quasi-norm-continuity" text="global"/>.</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-quasi-norm-continuity">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>(X,\norm{\cdot})</m> be a quasi-normed vector space.
Then
<md>\lim_{n\to+\infty}\norm{\alpha_nx_n - \alpha x} = 0</md>
if <m>\alpha_n \to \alpha</m> and <m>\norm{x_n-x} \to 0</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Put <m>P_n(\beta)=\norm{\beta(x_n-x)}</m>.
<em>Claim: for every <m>M&gt;0</m>, <m>P_n\to0</m> uniformly on <m>\set{\abs\beta\le M}</m>.</em>
Since <m>\abs{P_n(\beta)-P_n(\gamma)}\le\norm{(\beta-\gamma)(x_n-x)}</m>, each <m>P_n</m> is continuous, hence measurable, and <m>P_n(\beta)\to0</m> for every <m>\beta</m> by (v).
By Egorov's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-egorov" text="global"/>) on <m>[0,1]</m>, there is a measurable <m>A\subset[0,1]</m> with <m>\abs A&gt;0</m> on which <m>P_n\to0</m> uniformly.
By Problem III of Exam I, there is <m>\lambda&gt;0</m> such that every <m>\beta</m> with <m>\abs\beta\le\lambda</m> is a difference <m>\beta_1-\beta_2</m> of points of <m>A</m>, and then <m>P_n(\beta)\le P_n(\beta_1)+P_n(\beta_2)</m> by (ii) and (iii); so <m>P_n\to0</m> uniformly on <m>\set{\abs\beta\le\lambda}</m>.
For <m>\abs\beta\le M</m> and an integer <m>k\ge M/\lambda</m>, (ii) gives <m>P_n(\beta)\le kP_n(\beta/k)</m>, which proves the claim.</p>
<p>Now <m>\abs{\alpha_n}\le M</m> for some <m>M</m>, so by the claim and (iv),
<md>\norm{\alpha_nx_n-\alpha x}\le\norm{\alpha_n(x_n-x)}+\norm{(\alpha_n-\alpha)x}\to0.</md></p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-property-L">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\R</m> be a function.
We say that <m>f</m> has <em>property</em> <m>\mathscr L</m> on <m>E</m> if for every <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, there is a closed set <m>F\subset E</m> such that <m>\abs{E\setminus F}&lt;\eps</m> and <m>f|_F\colon F\to\R</m> is continuous.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-restriction-continuity">
<p>Recall that <m>f|_F\colon F\to\R</m> is continuous if <m>f(x_k)\to f(x_0)</m> for any <m>x_0\in F</m> and for any sequence <m>\set{x_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> in <m>F</m> converging to <m>x_0</m>.</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-lusin">
<title>Lusin</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\R</m> be a function.
Then <m>f</m> is a measurable function if and only if <m>f</m> has property <m>\mathscr L</m> on <m>E</m>.</p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<lemma xml:id="lem-simple-lusin">
<statement>
<p>Any measurable simple function has property <m>\mathscr L</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>f=\sum_{j=1}^{N}a_j1_{E_j}</m> and <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-inner-closed" text="global"/>, choose closed <m>F_j\subset E_j</m> with <m>\abs{E_j\setminus F_j}&lt;\eps/N</m>, and let <m>F=F_1\cup\cdots\cup F_N</m>.
Then <m>\abs{E\setminus F}&lt;\eps</m>, and <m>f</m> is constant on each of the finitely many disjoint closed sets <m>F_j</m>, so <m>f|_F</m> is continuous.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<proof ref="thm-lusin">
<p>Suppose <m>f</m> is measurable and first let <m>\abs{E}&lt;+\infty</m>.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-simple-approximation" text="global"/>, take measurable simple <m>f_k\to f</m>.
Given <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, Lemma <xref ref="lem-simple-lusin" text="global"/> gives closed <m>F_k</m> with <m>\abs{E\setminus F_k}&lt;\eps/2^{k+1}</m> and <m>f_k|_{F_k}</m> continuous, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-egorov" text="global"/> gives closed <m>F_0</m> with <m>\abs{E\setminus F_0}&lt;\eps/2</m> and <m>f_k\to f</m> uniformly on <m>F_0</m>.
On the closed set <m>F=\bigcap_{k\ge0}F_k</m>, <m>f</m> is a uniform limit of continuous functions, and <m>\abs{E\setminus F}&lt;\eps</m>.</p>
<p>If <m>\abs E=+\infty</m>, apply the finite case to <m>E_k=E\cap\set{k-1\le\abs x&lt;k}</m> to get closed <m>F_k\subset E_k</m> with <m>\abs{E_k\setminus F_k}&lt;\eps/2^k</m> and <m>f|_{F_k}</m> continuous.
Every bounded set meets only finitely many <m>F_k</m>, so <m>F=\bigcup_kF_k</m> is closed, <m>f|_F</m> is continuous, and <m>\abs{E\setminus F}&lt;\eps</m>.</p>
<p>Conversely, if <m>f</m> has property <m>\mathscr L</m>, choose closed <m>F_k\subset E</m> with <m>\abs{E\setminus F_k}&lt;1/k</m> and <m>f|_{F_k}</m> continuous.
Each <m>\set{x\in F_k\mid f(x)&gt;a}</m> is relatively open in the closed set <m>F_k</m>, hence measurable, and <m>Z=E\setminus\bigcup_kF_k</m> has measure zero.
So <m>\set{f&gt;a}=\bigcup_k\set{x\in F_k\mid f(x)&gt;a}\cup(\set{f&gt;a}\cap Z)</m> is measurable.</p>
</proof>
<aside>
<title>Supplement</title>
<p>Littlewood's three principles of real analysis:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There are three principles, roughly expressible in the following terms: Every (measurable) set is nearly a finite sum of intervals; every function (of class <m>L^p</m>) is nearly continuous; every convergent sequence of functions is nearly uniformly convergent.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The second principle is based on Lusin's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-lusin" text="global"/>), and the third is based on Egorov's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-egorov" text="global"/>).</p>
</aside>
</subsection>
</section>
<section xml:id="sec-integrals">
<title>Lebesgue Integrals</title>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1001">
<title>20261001</title>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset \R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative function, where <m>E</m> is a measurable set.
Define
<md>
<mrow>\Gamma(f,E) = \Gamma(f) &amp;\coloneqq \set{(x,f(x))\in\R^n\times \R=\R^{n+1}\mid x\in E,\, f(x)&lt;+\infty},</mrow>
<mrow>R(f,E) = R(f) &amp;\coloneqq \set{(x,y)\in\R^{n+1}\mid 0\le y\le f(x) \text{ if } f(x)&lt;+\infty,\, 0\le y&lt;+\infty \text{ if } f(x)=+\infty}.</mrow>
</md>
We call <m>\Gamma(f,E)</m> the <em>graph</em> of <m>f</m> (over <m>E</m>) and <m>R(f,E)</m> is <em>region</em> of <m>f</m> (over <m>E</m>).</p>
<definition xml:id="def-integral-nonnegative">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to \overline\R</m> be a nonnegative function.
If <m>R(f,E)</m> is measurable in <m>\R^{n+1}</m>, its measure <m>\abs{R(f,E)}</m> is called the <em>Lebesgue integral</em> of <m>f</m> over <m>E</m> and we write
<md>\abs{R(f,E)} = \int_{E} f(x)\,dx = \int_{E} f.</md>
We say that the Lebesgue integral <m>\int_E f</m> exists if <m>R(f,E)</m> is measurable.
(The value <m>\abs{R(f,E)}</m> can be <m>+\infty</m>.)</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<theorem xml:id="thm-measurable-integrable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative function.
If <m>f</m> is measurable, then <m>\int_E f</m> exists.</p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<p>To prove this, we need the following two lemmas.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-region-constant">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f(x)=a1_E(x)</m>, where <m>0\le a\le +\infty</m>.
Then <m>R(f,E)</m> is measurable and <m>\abs{R(f,E)}=a\abs{E}</m>.
Here we use the convention that <m>0\cdot +\infty = 0</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>For <m>a&lt;+\infty</m>, <m>R(f,E)=E\times[0,a]</m>, which is measurable with measure <m>a\abs{E}</m>.
For <m>a=+\infty</m>, <m>R(f,E)=\bigcup_k(E\times[0,k])</m>; apply Theorems <xref ref="thm-countable-union" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-continuity-measure" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-graph-null">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Then <m>\Gamma(f,E)</m> has measure zero.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>First let <m>\abs{E}&lt;+\infty</m> and <m>\eps&gt;0</m>.
The sets <m>E_k=\set{k\eps\le f&lt;(k+1)\eps}</m>, <m>k\in\N_0</m>, are disjoint and measurable with union <m>E\cap\set{f&lt;+\infty}</m>, and <m>\Gamma(f,E_k)\subset E_k\times[k\eps,(k+1)\eps]</m>.
Hence <m>\abs{\Gamma(f,E)}_e\le\sum_k\eps\abs{E_k}\le\eps\abs{E}</m>.
In general, <m>\Gamma(f,E)</m> is the countable union of the graphs over <m>E\cap\set{k\le\abs x&lt;k+1}</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<proof ref="thm-measurable-integrable">
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-simple-approximation" text="global"/>, take measurable simple functions <m>0\le f_k\nearrow f</m>.
Each <m>R(f_k,E)</m> is a finite union of sets as in Lemma <xref ref="lem-region-constant" text="global"/>, hence measurable, and <m>R(f_k,E)</m> increases with <m>k</m>.
A point <m>(x,y)\in R(f,E)</m> either lies on <m>\Gamma(f,E)</m> (if <m>y=f(x)</m>) or satisfies <m>y&lt;f(x)</m>, and then <m>y\le f_k(x)</m> for large <m>k</m>.
Hence
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-region-union" number="yes">R(f,E)=\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty}R(f_k,E)\right)\cup\Gamma(f,E),</mrow>
</md>
which is measurable by Lemma <xref ref="lem-graph-null" text="global"/>, and <m>\int_Ef=\lim_k\abs{R(f_k,E)}</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-continuity-measure" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
<corollary xml:id="cor-simple-integral">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f=\sum_{j=1}^{N}a_j1_{E_j}</m> be a nonnegative measurable simple function, where <m>a_j\ge 0</m>, the sets <m>\set{E_j}_{j=1}^{N}</m> are disjoint and measurable, and <m>a_j\ne a_\ell</m> for <m>j, \ell = 1,\ldots, N</m> and <m>j \ne \ell</m>.
Set <m>E=\bigcup_{j=1}^{N}E_j</m>.
Then
<md>\int_E f(x)\,dx=\sum_{j=1}^{N}a_j\abs{E_j}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>R(f,E)</m> is the disjoint union of the sets <m>R(a_j1_{E_j},E_j)</m>; apply Lemma <xref ref="lem-region-constant" text="global"/> and Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<lemma xml:id="lem-basic-properties">
<statement>
<p><ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
If <m>\abs{E}=0</m>, then <m>\int_E f=0</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be nonnegative measurable functions such that <m>g\le f</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>.
Then <m>\int_E g\le \int_E f</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Let <m>E_1,E_2\subset\R^n</m> be measurable sets with <m>E_1\subset E_2</m>, and let <m>f\colon E_2\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Then <m>\int_{E_1} f \le \int_{E_2} f</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
If <m>\int_E f&lt;+\infty</m>, then <m>f(x)&lt;+\infty</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>(i) For simple <m>0\le f_k\nearrow f</m>, <m>\int_Ef_k=0</m> by Corollary <xref ref="cor-simple-integral" text="global"/>, so <m>\int_Ef=\lim_k\int_Ef_k=0</m> by the proof of Theorem <xref ref="thm-measurable-integrable" text="global"/>.</p>
<p>(ii) With <m>Z=\set{g&gt;f}</m>, we have <m>R(g,E)\subset R(f,E)\cup R(g,Z)</m>, and <m>\abs{R(g,Z)}=0</m> by (i).</p>
<p>(iii) <m>R(f,E_1)\subset R(f,E_2)</m>.</p>
<p>(iv) Let <m>E_\infty=\set{f=+\infty}</m>.
For every <m>a&gt;0</m>, (ii), (iii) and Corollary <xref ref="cor-simple-integral" text="global"/> give <m>\int_Ef\ge\int_{E_\infty}a=a\abs{E_\infty}</m>, so <m>\abs{E_\infty}=0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-monotone-convergence">
<title>Monotone convergence theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function for each <m>k\in\N</m>.
Assume that <m>f_1(x)\le f_2(x)\le \cdots</m> and <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty}f_k(x)=f(x)</m> on <m>E</m>.
Then
<md>\lim_{k\to+\infty} \int_{E} f_k = \int_{E} f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>f</m> is measurable by Theorem <xref ref="thm-limsup-measurable" text="global"/>.
The argument for <xref ref="eq-region-union" text="global"/> applies verbatim to <m>f_k</m>, so <m>R(f_k,E)\nearrow R(f,E)</m> up to the null set <m>\Gamma(f,E)</m>, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-continuity-measure" text="global"/> gives the claim.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1006">
<title>20261006</title>
<theorem xml:id="thm-integral-countable-additivity">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset\R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Suppose that <m>E = \bigcup_{j=1}^{+\infty}E_j</m>, where <m>\set{E_j}_{j=1}^{+\infty}</m> are disjoint measurable sets.
Then
<md>\int_E f=\sum_{j=1}^{+\infty}\int_{E_j}f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>R(f,E)</m> is the disjoint union of the measurable sets <m>R(f,E_j)</m>; apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-additivity" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-integral-supremum">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset\R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Then
<md>\int_E f=\sup\left(\sum_{j=1}^{N}\left(\inf_{x\in E_j}f(x)\right)\abs{E_j}\right),</md>
where the supremum is taken over all decompositions <m>E=\bigcup_{j=1}^{N}E_j</m> of <m>E</m> into a finite number of disjoint measurable sets <m>E_j</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>For a decomposition <m>\set{E_j}</m>, the simple function <m>g=\sum_j(\inf_{E_j}f)1_{E_j}</m> satisfies <m>g\le f</m>, so its integral, the sum in question, is at most <m>\int_Ef</m> by Corollary <xref ref="cor-simple-integral" text="global"/> and Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>.
Conversely, the simple functions <m>f_k\nearrow f</m> of Theorem <xref ref="thm-simple-approximation" text="global"/> have the form <m>\sum_jc_j1_{E_j}</m> with <m>c_j\le\inf_{E_j}f</m>, so each <m>\int_Ef_k</m> is bounded by the supremum; let <m>k\to+\infty</m> using Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-zero-integral">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\subset \R^n\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Then <m>\int_E f=0</m> if and only if <m>f=0</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>If <m>f=0</m> a.e., then <m>\int_Ef\le\int_E0=0</m> by Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(ii).
Conversely, if <m>\int_Ef=0</m>, then for <m>E_k=\set{f&gt;1/k}</m> we get <m>\frac1k\abs{E_k}\le\int_Ef=0</m>, and <m>\set{f\ne0}=\bigcup_kE_k</m> has measure zero.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-chebyshev">
<title>Tchebyshev's inequality</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Let <m>\alpha&gt;0</m>.
Then
<md>\abs{\set{x\in E\mid f(x)&gt;\alpha}} \le \frac{1}{\alpha}\int_E f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>\alpha1_{\set{f&gt;\alpha}}\le f</m>; integrate using Corollary <xref ref="cor-simple-integral" text="global"/> and Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(ii).</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-linearity-nonnegative">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be nonnegative measurable functions.
Then
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>\int_E cf = c\int_E f</m> for any <m>c\ge 0</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\int_E (f+g)=\int_E f+ \int_E g</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>For simple functions both identities follow from Corollary <xref ref="cor-simple-integral" text="global"/>; for (ii), write <m>f+g=\sum_{j,\ell}(a_j+b_\ell)1_{E_j\cap\widehat E_\ell}</m> when <m>f=\sum_ja_j1_{E_j}</m> and <m>g=\sum_\ell b_\ell1_{\widehat E_\ell}</m>.
In general, take simple <m>f_k\nearrow f</m> and <m>g_k\nearrow g</m>; then <m>cf_k\nearrow cf</m> and <m>f_k+g_k\nearrow f+g</m>, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/> passes the identities to the limit.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-integral-difference">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be nonnegative measurable functions.
Suppose that <m>f\le g</m> and <m>\int_E f&lt;+\infty</m>.
Then
<md>\int_E (g-f) = \int_E g - \int_E f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-linearity-nonnegative" text="global"/>(ii) to <m>g=(g-f)+f</m> and subtract the finite number <m>\int_Ef</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<corollary xml:id="cor-series-integral">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be nonnegative measurable functions.
Then
<md>\int_E \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}f_k = \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\int_E f_k.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/> to the partial sums, using Theorem <xref ref="thm-linearity-nonnegative" text="global"/>(ii).</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fatou-nonnegative">
<title>Fatou's lemma for nonnegative functions</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be nonnegative measurable functions.
Then
<md>\int_E \liminf_{k\to+\infty}f_k \le \liminf_{k\to+\infty} \int_E f_k.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>g_k=\inf_{j\ge k}f_j</m>.
Then <m>g_k\nearrow\liminf_kf_k</m> and <m>g_k\le f_k</m>, so by Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/> and Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(ii),
<md>\int_E\liminf_{k\to+\infty}f_k=\lim_{k\to+\infty}\int_Eg_k\le\liminf_{k\to+\infty}\int_Ef_k.</md></p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-dct-nonnegative">
<title>Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem for nonnegative functions</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be nonnegative measurable functions.
Assume that <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty}f_k=f</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>, where <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> is a nonnegative measurable function.
If there is an integrable function <m>\phi\colon E\to\overline\R</m> such that <m>f_k\le\phi</m> for all <m>k</m> and <m>\int_E\phi&lt;+\infty</m>, then
<md>\lim_{k\to+\infty}\int_E f_k = \int_E f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Theorem <xref ref="thm-fatou-nonnegative" text="global"/> applied to <m>f_k</m> and to <m>\phi-f_k\ge0</m>, together with Corollary <xref ref="cor-integral-difference" text="global"/>, gives
<md>\int_Ef\le\liminf_{k\to+\infty}\int_Ef_k
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        \int_E\phi-\int_Ef\le\int_E\phi-\limsup_{k\to+\infty}\int_Ef_k.</md>
Since <m>\int_E\phi&lt;+\infty</m>, the claim follows.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>Let us revisit everyone's favorite function in Calculus, now with the theory of Lebesgue integrals in hand:</p>
<example xml:id="ex-stirling">
<p>For <m>\lambda\ge 1</m>, the gamma function is defined by
<md>\Gamma(\lambda+1) \coloneqq
        \int_{0}^{+\infty} e^{-t}t^{\lambda} \,dt.</md>
Recall that <m>\Gamma(n+1)=n!</m> for <m>n\in\N_0</m>.
The change of variable <m>t=\lambda(1+s)</m> yields
<md>\Gamma(\lambda+1)
        = \int_{0}^{+\infty} e^{-t}t^{\lambda} \,dt
        = \int_{-1}^{+\infty} e^{-\lambda(1+s)}(\lambda(1+s))^\lambda \,ds
        = e^{-\lambda}\lambda^{\lambda+1}\int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda(s-\log(1+s))}.</md>
Letting <m>F(s) = s-\log(1+s)</m>, we have
<md>\frac{\Gamma(\lambda+1)}{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^{\lambda+1}}
        = \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda(s-\log(1+s))}\,ds
        = \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda F(s)}\,ds.</md>
Note that <m>F(0)=0</m>, <m>F'(0)=0</m>, and <m>F''(0)=1</m>, so by Morse lemma, there exists a smooth bijective function <m>\phi:(-\eps,\eps)\to (-\delta,\delta)</m> with <m>\phi(0)=0</m>, <m>\phi'(0)=1</m>, and <m>\phi'&gt;0</m>, such that <m>F(\phi(s))=s^2/2</m> for all <m>s\in(-\eps,\eps)</m>.
Let <m>\chi\in C^\infty(\R)</m> be the cutoff function such that <m>0\le \chi\le 1</m> and
<md>
<mrow>\chi(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            1 &amp; x\in [-\delta/2,\delta/2] \\
            0 &amp; x\notin (-\delta,\delta)
        \end{cases}.</mrow>
</md>
Then
<md>\frac{\Gamma(\lambda+1)}{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^{\lambda+1}}
        = I(\lambda) + II(\lambda),</md>
where
<md>I(\lambda) = \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda F(s)}\chi(s)\,ds, \quad
        II(\lambda) = \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda F(s)}(1-\chi(s))\,ds.</md>
Note that
<md>
<mrow>I(\lambda)
        &amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda F(\phi(s))}\chi(\phi(s))\phi'(s)\,ds</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda s^2/2}\chi(\phi(s))\phi'(s)\,ds</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{- s^2/2}\chi(\phi(s/\sqrt{\lambda}))\phi'(s/\sqrt{\lambda})\frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda}}1_{[-\sqrt{\lambda},+\infty]}\,ds.</mrow>
</md>
Hence, by Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct-nonnegative" text="global"/>),
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt{\lambda}I(\lambda)
        &amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{- s^2/2}\chi(\phi(s/\sqrt{\lambda}))\phi'(s/\sqrt{\lambda})1_{[-\sqrt{\lambda},+\infty]}\,ds</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;\to \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}e^{-s^2/2}\,ds = \sqrt{2\pi}</mrow>
</md>
as <m>\lambda\to+\infty</m>.
It follows that
<md>I(\lambda)
        = \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}} +
        o(1/\sqrt{\lambda})
        = \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}} + \frac{\eps_j}{\sqrt{\lambda}}</md>
for some <m>\eps_j\to 0</m>.
On the other hand, we have
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt{\lambda} II(\lambda)
        &amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty} e^{-\lambda F(s)}\sqrt{\lambda}(1-\chi(s)) \,ds</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty}e^{-\lambda F(s)}\sqrt{\lambda}(1-\chi(s)) 1_{S_\delta}\,ds,</mrow>
</md>
where <m>S_\delta = [-1,+\infty)\setminus [-\delta/2,\delta/2]</m>.
We can show that <m>F(x) \ge c(1+s_+)</m> on <m>S_\delta</m>, where <m>c&gt;0</m> is a constant and <m>s_+ = \max\set{0,s}</m>.
Since <m>\lim_{\lambda\to +\infty} e^{-\lambda c(1+s_+)} \sqrt{\lambda} = 0</m> for all <m>s \in S_\delta</m>, we have
<md>\sqrt{\lambda} e^{-\lambda F(s)} \le C e^{-F(s)}</md>
on <m>S_\delta</m>, where <m>C&gt;0</m> is a constant and <m>\int_{-1}^{+\infty} e^{-F(s)} &lt; +\infty</m>.
Therefore, we may apply Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct-nonnegative" text="global"/>) and this gives us
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{\lambda\to+\infty} \sqrt{\lambda}II(\lambda)
    &amp;= \int_{-1}^{+\infty} \lim_{\lambda\to+\infty}e^{-\lambda F(s)}\sqrt{\lambda}(1-\chi(s)) 1_{S_\delta}\,ds</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;\le \int_{-1}^{+\infty} \lim_{\lambda\to+\infty}e^{-\lambda c(1+s_+)}\sqrt{\lambda}(1-\chi(s)) 1_{S_\delta}\,ds</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= 0,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>II(\lambda)=\widehat\eps_j/\sqrt{\lambda}</m> for some <m>\widehat\eps_j\to 0</m>.
In conclusion,
<md>\frac{\Gamma(\lambda+1)}{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^{\lambda+1}}
        = I(\lambda) + II(\lambda)
        = \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}} + \frac{\widetilde\eps_j}{\sqrt{\lambda}}</md>
for some <m>\widetilde\eps_j\to 0</m>.
This gives us the asymptotic approximation
<md>
<mrow>\Gamma(\lambda+1) &amp;= e^{-\lambda} \lambda^{\lambda+1} \left( \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}} + \frac{\widetilde\eps_j}{\sqrt{\lambda}}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;=\left(\frac{\lambda}{e}\right)^\lambda(\sqrt{2\pi\lambda} + \sqrt{\lambda}\widetilde\eps_j)</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;=\left(\frac{\lambda}{e}\right)^\lambda\sqrt{2\pi\lambda} (1+\eps'_j),</mrow>
</md>
where <m>\eps'_j\to 0</m>.
In particular, we have the Stirling formula
<md>\Gamma(n+1) = n! \sim \left(\frac{n}{e}\right)^n\sqrt{2\pi n}.</md></p>
</example>
<remark xml:id="rmk-asymptotic-expansion">
<p><ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>In fact, we can show that
<md>\Gamma(\lambda+1) = e^{-\lambda} \lambda^{\lambda+1}\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}} (1+a_1 \lambda^{-1} + a_2 \lambda^{-2} + \ldots)</md>
with distribution theory and Fourier analysis. We will introduce them in the next semester.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Also, we will introduce the oscillatory integrals
<md>\int e^{i\lambda\phi(x)} u(x)\, dx,</md>
Feynman integrals, and WKB method.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</remark>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1008">
<title>20261008</title>
<p>So far, we have only considered nonnegative measurable functions.
We will now generalize the theory to any measurable function <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m>.
Let <m>f_+ \coloneqq \max\set{f,0}</m> and <m>f_-\coloneqq -\min\set{f,0}</m>.</p>
<definition xml:id="def-integral-general">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
We say that the Lebesgue integral of <m>f</m> over <m>E</m> exists if <m>\int_E f_+&lt;+\infty</m> or <m>\int_E f_-&lt;+\infty</m>.
In this case, we write
<md>\int_E f = \int_E f(x)\,dx \coloneqq \int_E f_+ - \int_E f_-,</md>
and we say that <m>f</m> is <em>Lebesgue integrable</em>.
Let
<md>L(E) = L^1(E)
        \coloneqq \left\lbrace f\colon E\to\overline\R: \text{measurable} \mid \int_E \abs{f}&lt;+\infty \right\rbrace.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>Later, we will consider spaces such as <m>L^2(E)</m> and <m>L^p(E)</m>, which possess richer structures.
The space <m>L^2(E)</m> is a Hilbert space and will be the primary object of study in Fourier analysis.</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-absolute-integrability">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
Then, <m>f\in L(E)</m> if and only if <m>\abs{f}\in L(E)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<remark xml:id="rmk-integral-triangle">
<p>If <m>\int_E f</m> exists, then
<md>\left\lvert \int_E f \right\rvert
        = \left\lvert \int_E f_+ - \int_E f_- \right\rvert
        \le \int_E f_+ + \int_E f_-
        = \int_E \abs{f}.</md></p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-integral-properties">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>If <m>\abs{E}=0</m> or <m>f=0</m> a.e., then <m>\int_E f=0</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If <m>\int_E f</m> and <m>\int_E g</m> exist and <m>f\le g</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>, then <m>\int_E f\le \int_E g</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If <m>\int_E f</m> exists and <m>E_1\subset E</m> is a measurable set, then <m>\int_{E_1} f</m> exists.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If <m>\int_E f</m> exists and <m>E=\bigcup_{k=1}^{+\infty} E_k</m>, where <m>\set{E_k}_{k=1}^{+\infty}</m> is a collection of disjoint measurable sets, then <m>\int_E f = \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\int_{E_k} f</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply the results for nonnegative functions to <m>f_\pm</m> and <m>g_\pm</m>:
(i) follows from Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(i) and Theorem <xref ref="thm-zero-integral" text="global"/>;
(ii) from <m>f_+\le g_+</m> and <m>g_-\le f_-</m> a.e. and Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(ii);
(iii) from Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(iii);
(iv) from Theorem <xref ref="thm-integral-countable-additivity" text="global"/>, subtracting the two series, at least one of which converges.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-integral-scalar">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
If <m>\int_E f</m> exists, then for any <m>c\in\R</m>, <m>\int_E cf</m> exists and <m>\int_E cf = c\int_E f</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>If <m>c\ge0</m>, then <m>(cf)_\pm=cf_\pm</m>; if <m>c&lt;0</m>, then <m>(cf)_\pm=\abs cf_\mp</m>.
In both cases use Theorem <xref ref="thm-linearity-nonnegative" text="global"/>(i).</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-integral-linearity">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
If <m>f,g\in L(E)</m>, then <m>f+g\in L(E)</m> and
<md>\int_E (f+g) = \int_E f + \int_E g.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Since <m>\abs{f+g}\le\abs f+\abs g</m>, <m>f+g\in L(E)</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-absolute-integrability" text="global"/>.
From <m>(f+g)_+-(f+g)_-=f_+-f_-+g_+-g_-</m> we get
<md>(f+g)_++f_-+g_-=(f+g)_-+f_++g_+,</md>
and integrating with Theorem <xref ref="thm-linearity-nonnegative" text="global"/>(ii) (all terms finite) gives the claim.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fatou">
<title>Fatou's lemma</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
If there exists <m>\phi\in L(E)</m> such that <m>f_k\ge \phi</m> on <m>E</m> for all <m>k\in \N</m>, then
<md>\int_E \liminf_{k\to+\infty} f_k \le \liminf_{k\to+\infty} \int_E f_k.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-fatou-nonnegative" text="global"/> to <m>f_k-\phi\ge0</m> and add <m>\int_E\phi</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-integral-linearity" text="global"/>).</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-reverse-fatou">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
If there exists <m>\phi\in L(E)</m> such that <m>f_k\le \phi</m> for all <m>k\in\N</m>, then
<md>\int_E \limsup_{k\to+\infty} f_k \ge \limsup_{k\to+\infty} \int_E f_k.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-fatou" text="global"/> to <m>-f_k\ge-\phi</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<theorem xml:id="thm-dct">
<title>Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions.
Suppose that <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty} f_k = f</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>, and there exists <m>\phi\in L(E)</m> such that <m>\abs{f_k}\le \phi</m> a.e. on <m>E</m> for all <m>k\in\N</m>.
Then
<md>\lim_{k\to+\infty} \int_E f_k = \int_E f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct-nonnegative" text="global"/> to <m>0\le f_k+\phi\le2\phi</m> and subtract <m>\int_E\phi</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-bounded-convergence">
<title>Bounded convergence theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be measurable functions for some <m>\abs{E}&lt;+\infty</m>.
Suppose that <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty} f_k = f</m> a.e. on <m>E</m>, and there exists a constant <m>C&gt;0</m> independent of <m>k</m> such that <m>\abs{f_k}\le C</m> for all <m>k\in\N</m>.
Then
<md>\lim_{k\to+\infty}\int_E f_k = \int_E f.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Take <m>\phi=C</m> in Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>; it is integrable since <m>\abs E&lt;+\infty</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<corollary xml:id="cor-riemann-lebesgue">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>I\subset\R^n</m> be an interval.
Let <m>f\colon I\to\R</m> be a bounded Riemann integrable function.
Then <m>f</m> is Lebesgue integrable on <m>I</m> and
<md>\text{R}\int_I f = \int_I f,</md>
where <m>\text{R}\int_I f</m> is the Riemann integral of <m>f</m> over <m>I</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Lebesgue's criterion, <m>f</m> is continuous a.e., hence measurable (Lemma <xref ref="lem-ae-measurable" text="global"/>), and bounded, hence in <m>L(I)</m>.
Let <m>P_k=\set{I_{k,1},\ldots,I_{k,N_k}}</m> be partitions of <m>I</m> with mesh <m>\le1/k</m> and put <m>g_k=\sum_j(\inf_{I_{k,j}}f)1_{I_{k,j}}</m>.
Then <m>\int_Ig_k</m> are lower Riemann sums, so they tend to <m>\text{R}\int_If</m>, while <m>g_k\to f</m> at every continuity point of <m>f</m>, i.e. a.e.
Corollary <xref ref="cor-bounded-convergence" text="global"/> gives <m>\int_Ig_k\to\int_If</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<example xml:id="ex-gauss-green">
<title>Gauss-Green theorem</title>
<p>Let <m>\Omega=\set{x\in\R^n \mid r(x)&lt;0}</m> be a bounded open set (also called a domain), where <m>r\in C^\infty(\R^n,\R)</m>.
Suppose that <m>\nabla r = (\pr r/\pr x_1,\dots, \pr r/\pr x_n) \ne 0</m> on <m>\pr\Omega = \set{x\in\R^n \mid r(x) =0}</m>.
Let <m>f\in C^\infty(\R^n)</m>.
Fix <m>j\in\set{1,2,\dots,n}</m> and fix <m>p\in\pr\Omega</m>.
Assume that <m>\pr r/\pr x_1(p) &lt; 0</m>.
By implicit function theorem, there exists an open neighborhood <m>U</m> of <m>p</m> and a function <m>\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)\in C^\infty(U)</m> such that
<md>
<mrow>\Omega \cap U &amp;= \set{x=(x_1,\dots,x_n)\in U \mid x_1&gt; \varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)},</mrow>
<mrow>\pr \Omega \cap U &amp;= \set{x=(x_1,\dots,x_n)\in U \mid x_1= \varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)}.</mrow>
</md>
Assume that <m>f\in C_c^\infty(U)</m>.
Then
<md>\int_{\Omega}\frac{\pr f}{\pr x_j} = \int_{\set{x_1&gt;\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)}} \frac{\pr f}{\pr x_j}.</md>
Take <m>h(x)\in C^\infty(\R)</m> such that <m>h=1</m> on <m>(1,+\infty)</m> and <m>h=0</m> on <m>(-\infty,0)</m>.
Then
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{\eps\to 0^+} h\left(\frac{x_1-\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)}{\eps}\right)
        =
        \begin{cases}
            1 &amp; \text{if } x_1 &gt; \varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n) \\
            0 &amp; \text{if } x_1 \le  \varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)
        \end{cases}.</mrow>
</md>
By Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>),
<md>\int_{\set{x_1&gt;\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)}} \frac{\pr f}{\pr x_j}
        = \lim_{\eps\to 0^+}\int_{\R^n} \frac{\pr f}{\pr x_j} h\left(\frac{x_1-\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)}{\eps}\right) \,dx.</md>
Using integration by parts, this is equal to
<md>
<mrow>&amp; -\lim_{\eps\to 0^+} \int_{\R^n} f(x)  h'\left(\frac{x_1-\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n)}{\eps}\right)V_j(x) \frac{1}{\eps} \,dx</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= -\lim_{\eps\to 0^+} \int_{\R^n} f(\eps y_1 + \varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n),x_2,\dots,x_n)  h'(y_1)</mrow>
<mrow>&amp; \hspace{4.5cm}  V_j(\eps y_1+\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n),x_2,\dots,x_n) \frac{1}{\eps} \,dy_1\,dx_2\cdots\,dx_n</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;= -\int_{\R^{n-1}} f(\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n),x_2,\dots,x_n) V_j(\varphi(x_2,\dots,x_n),x_2,\dots,x_n) \,dx_2\cdots \,dx_n.</mrow>
</md>
where <m>V=\left(1,-\frac{\pr\varphi}{\pr x_2},\ldots,
-\frac{\pr\varphi}{\pr x_n}\right)
=\bigl(V_1(x),\ldots,V_n(x)\bigr).</m>
Note that
<md>\frac{V}{\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{\pr\varphi}{\pr x_2}\right)^2
        +\cdots+\left(\frac{\pr\varphi}{\pr x_n}\right)^2}}
        = (N_1,\ldots,N_n)</md>
is the inward unit normal vector, and
<md>dS=\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{\pr\varphi}{\pr x_2}\right)^2
        +\cdots+\left(\frac{\pr\varphi}{\pr x_n}\right)^2}
        \,dx_2\cdots dx_n</md>
is the Euclidean surface element.
Hence,
<md>\int_\Omega\frac{\pr f}{\pr x_j}
        =-\int_{\pr\Omega}fN_j\,dS.</md>
Let <m>f=(f_1,\ldots,f_n)</m>, where <m>f_j\in C^\infty(\R^n)</m> for each <m>j</m>.
Then from the Gauss-Green theorem, we obtain the Gauss-Green formula:
<md>
<mrow>\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}f
        &amp;=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\int_\Omega\frac{\pr f_j}{\pr x_j}</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}\int_{\pr\Omega}f_jN_j\,dS</mrow>
<mrow>&amp;=-\int_{\pr\Omega}\ags{f,N}\,dS.</mrow>
</md></p>
</example>
<question xml:id="q-gauss-green-ae">
<p>If <m>\nabla r\ne0</m> a.e. on <m>\pr\Omega</m>, would the Gauss-Green formula remain true?</p>
</question>
</subsection>
</section>
<section xml:id="sec-fubini">
<title>Repeated Integrations</title>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1013">
<title>20261013</title>
<p>The goal of this chapter is to prove Fubini's theorem.
Consider <m>\R^{n+m}=\R^n\times \R^m</m>, and let <m>x=(x_1,\dots,x_n)</m> (resp. <m>y=(y_1,\dots,y_m)</m>) be the coordinates of <m>\R^n</m> (resp. <m>\R^m</m>).</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fubini-1">
<title>Fubini's theorem, version 1</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon\R^{n+m}\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
Suppose that <m>f\in L(\R^{n+m})</m>, i.e.,
<md>\int_{\R^{n+m}}\abs{f(x,y)}&lt;+\infty.</md>
Then
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>for all <m>x\in\R^n\setminus Z</m>, where <m>Z</m> is some measure zero set, <m>f(x,y)</m> is measurable and integrable on <m>\R^m</m>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the function <m>x\in \R^n\setminus Z \mapsto \int_{\R^m}f(x,y)\,dy</m> is measurable and integrable on <m>\R^n</m>, and</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><md>\int_{\R^n}\int_{\R^m}f(x,y)\,dy\,dx = \int_{\R^{n+m}}f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.</md></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-property-F">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\in L(\R^{n+m})</m>.
We say that <m>f</m> satisfies property <m>\mathscr F</m> if Fubini's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-fubini-1" text="global"/>) holds for <m>f</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fubini-linear">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_j\in L(\R^{n+m})</m> for <m>j=1,\dots,N</m>.
Suppose that <m>f_j</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m> for every <m>j</m>.
Then any linear combination of <m>f_1,\dots,f_N</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>f=\sum_j\lambda_jf_j</m>; then <m>f\in L(\R^{n+m})</m> by Theorems <xref ref="thm-integral-scalar" text="global"/> and <xref ref="thm-integral-linearity" text="global"/>.
Let <m>Z_j</m> be the exceptional set for <m>f_j</m> and <m>Z=\bigcup_jZ_j</m>.
For <m>x\notin Z</m>, every <m>f_j(x,\cdot)</m> is integrable, hence finite a.e., so <m>f(x,\cdot)</m> is defined a.e., measurable and integrable, with
<md>\int_{\R^m}f(x,y)\,dy=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\lambda_j\int_{\R^m}f_j(x,y)\,dy.</md>
This gives (i) and (ii), and (iii) follows by linearity of both sides of (iii) for each <m>f_j</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fubini-monotone">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f_k\in L(\R^{n+m})</m> for each <m>k\in\N</m>.
Suppose that <m>f_k</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m> for every <m>k</m>, and <m>f_k</m> monotonically converges to <m>f</m>.
Then <m>f</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m> if <m>f\in L(\R^{n+m})</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-linear" text="global"/> applied to <m>-f_k</m>, it suffices to treat <m>f_k\nearrow f</m>.
Let <m>Z_0=\bigcup_kZ_k</m> be the union of the exceptional sets, and put <m>g_k=f_k-f_1\nearrow g=f-f_1\ge0</m>; the <m>g_k</m> have property <m>\mathscr F</m> by Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-linear" text="global"/>.
For <m>x\notin Z_0</m>, <m>g(x,\cdot)</m> is measurable (Theorem <xref ref="thm-limsup-measurable" text="global"/>) and, by Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/>,
<md>h_k(x)\coloneqq\int_{\R^m}g_k(x,y)\,dy\nearrow\int_{\R^m}g(x,y)\,dy\eqqcolon h(x),</md>
so <m>h</m> is measurable.
Applying Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/> in <m>\R^n</m> and in <m>\R^{n+m}</m>,
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-fubini-monotone" number="yes">\int_{\R^n}h=\lim_{k\to+\infty}\int_{\R^n}h_k=\lim_{k\to+\infty}\int_{\R^{n+m}}g_k=\int_{\R^{n+m}}g&lt;+\infty.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>h&lt;+\infty</m> outside a null set <m>Z\supset Z_0</m> (Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(iv)), and for <m>x\notin Z</m> the function <m>f(x,\cdot)=g(x,\cdot)+f_1(x,\cdot)</m> is integrable, with <m>\int_{\R^m}f(x,y)\,dy=h(x)+\int_{\R^m}f_1(x,y)\,dy</m>.
This proves (i) and (ii), and (iii) follows from <xref ref="eq-fubini-monotone" text="global"/> and property <m>\mathscr F</m> of <m>f_1</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fubini-gdelta">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E=\bigcap_{k=1}^{+\infty}G_k\subset\R^{n+m}</m>, where <m>G_k</m> is open for every <m>k</m> and one of them has finite measure.
Then <m>1_E(x,y)</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>In each step, <m>\abs{E}&lt;+\infty</m>, so <m>1_E\in L(\R^{n+m})</m>.
Write <m>E_x=\set{y\mid(x,y)\in E}</m>.</p>
<p><em>Step 1: <m>E=I_1\times I_2</m> is an interval.</em>
Then <m>1_E(x,y)=1_{I_1}(x)1_{I_2}(y)</m>, so <m>\int_{\R^m}1_E(x,y)\,dy=\abs{I_2}1_{I_1}(x)</m> and both sides of (iii) equal <m>\abs{I_1}\abs{I_2}=\abs E</m>.</p>
<p><em>Step 2: <m>E\subset\bigcup_j\pr I_j</m> for intervals <m>I_j=J_{1,j}\times J_{2,j}</m>.</em>
Then <m>\abs E=0</m> by Lemma <xref ref="lem-boundary-null" text="global"/> and Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>.
For <m>x</m> outside the null set <m>\bigcup_j\pr J_{1,j}</m>, we have <m>E_x\subset\bigcup_j\pr J_{2,j}</m>, a null set.
So <m>\int_{\R^m}1_E(x,y)\,dy=0</m> for a.e. <m>x</m>, and both sides of (iii) vanish.</p>
<p><em>Step 3: <m>E</m> is open with <m>\abs E&lt;+\infty</m>.</em>
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-open-decomposition" text="global"/>, <m>E=\bigcup_jI_j</m> with nonoverlapping closed cubes <m>I_j</m>.
The sets <m>E_0=\bigcup_j\pr I_j</m> and <m>\mathrm{int}(I_k)</m>, <m>k\in\N</m>, are disjoint with union <m>E</m>, so
<md>1_{E_0}+\sum_{k=1}^{N}1_{\mathrm{int}(I_k)}\nearrow1_E.</md>
Each term has property <m>\mathscr F</m> by Steps 1 and 2 (an open interval differs from a closed one by a set as in Step 2), so <m>1_E</m> has it by Lemmas <xref ref="lem-fubini-linear" text="global"/> and <xref ref="lem-fubini-monotone" text="global"/>.</p>
<p><em>Step 4: the general case.</em>
Assume <m>\abs{G_1}&lt;+\infty</m>.
Then <m>E_k=G_1\cap\cdots\cap G_k</m> are open with finite measure, and <m>1_{E_k}\searrow1_E</m>; apply Step 3 and Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-monotone" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fubini-null">
<statement>
<p>If <m>Z</m> is a measure zero set in <m>\R^{n+m}</m>, then <m>1_Z</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Choose open <m>G_j\supset Z</m> with <m>\abs{G_j}&lt;1/j</m>, and let <m>H=\bigcap_jG_j</m>, so <m>Z\subset H</m> and <m>\abs H=0</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-gdelta" text="global"/>, <m>\int_{\R^n}\int_{\R^m}1_H(x,y)\,dy\,dx=\abs H=0</m>, so by Theorem <xref ref="thm-zero-integral" text="global"/> (twice) the slice <m>H_x</m> is null for a.e. <m>x</m>.
Hence <m>Z_x\subset H_x</m> is null for a.e. <m>x</m>, and both sides of (iii) for <m>1_Z</m> vanish.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fubini-finite-measure">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E\subset\R^{n+m}</m> be a measurable set with <m>\abs{E}&lt;+\infty</m>.
Then <m>1_E</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-gdelta-fsigma-char" text="global"/>(i) (with the open sets chosen of finite measure), <m>E=H\setminus Z</m> with <m>H</m> as in Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-gdelta" text="global"/> and <m>\abs Z=0</m>.
So <m>1_E=1_H-1_Z</m>; apply Lemmas <xref ref="lem-fubini-gdelta" text="global"/>, <xref ref="lem-fubini-null" text="global"/> and <xref ref="lem-fubini-linear" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<proof ref="thm-fubini-1">
<p>By Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-linear" text="global"/>, it suffices to treat <m>f_+</m>, and similarly <m>f_-</m>.
Take simple <m>0\le f_k\nearrow f_+</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-simple-approximation" text="global"/>), <m>f_k=\sum_ja_{k,j}1_{E_{k,j}}</m> with <m>a_{k,j}&gt;0</m>.
Since <m>a_{k,j}\abs{E_{k,j}}\le\int f_+&lt;+\infty</m>, each <m>\abs{E_{k,j}}</m> is finite, so <m>f_k</m> has property <m>\mathscr F</m> by Lemmas <xref ref="lem-fubini-finite-measure" text="global"/> and <xref ref="lem-fubini-linear" text="global"/>.
Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-monotone" text="global"/> finishes the proof.</p>
</proof>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1015">
<title>20261015</title>
<lemma xml:id="lem-slice-measurable-set">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E\subset \R^{n+m}</m> be any measurable set.
Then for almost every <m>x\in \R^n</m>, the set
<md>E_x = \set{y\in\R^m \mid (x,y)\in E}</md>
is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>E_k=E\cap\set{\abs z&lt;k}</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-fubini-finite-measure" text="global"/>, <m>1_{E_k}(x,\cdot)</m> is measurable, i.e. <m>(E_k)_x</m> is measurable, for <m>x</m> outside a null set <m>Z_k</m>.
For <m>x\notin\bigcup_kZ_k</m>, <m>E_x=\bigcup_k(E_k)_x</m> is measurable.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-slice-measurable">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f(x,y)\colon \R^{n+m}\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
Then for almost every <m>x\in\R^n</m>, the function <m>y\in\R^m\mapsto f(x,y)\in\overline\R</m> is measurable.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Lemma <xref ref="lem-slice-measurable-set" text="global"/> to the countably many sets <m>\set{f&gt;r}</m>, <m>r\in\Q</m>, and discard the union <m>Z</m> of the exceptional sets.
For <m>x\notin Z</m> and <m>a\in\R</m>, <m>\set{y\mid f(x,y)&gt;a}=\bigcup_{r\in\Q,\,r&gt;a}\set{y\mid f(x,y)&gt;r}</m> is measurable.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fubini-2">
<title>Fubini's theorem, version 2</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E\subset\R^{n+m}</m> be a measurable set and let <m>f(x,y)\colon E\to\overline\R</m> be a measurable function.
For each <m>x\in\R^n</m>, let
<md>E_x = \set{y\in\R^m \mid (x,y)\in E}.</md>
Then we have the following.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>For almost every <m>x\in\R^n</m>, <m>f(x,y)</m> is measurable on <m>E_x</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Assume that <m>f\in L(E)</m>.
Then <m>f(x,y)\in L(E_x)</m> for almost every <m>x\in\R^n</m>, and <m>x\in\R^n\setminus Z\mapsto \int_{E_x}f(x,y)\,dy</m> is integrable for some measure zero set <m>Z</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><md>\int_E f = \int_{\R^n}\int_{E_x}f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.</md></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Extend <m>f</m> by zero to <m>\widetilde f\colon\R^{n+m}\to\overline\R</m>; it is measurable since <m>\set{\widetilde f&gt;a}</m> is <m>\set{f&gt;a}</m> or <m>\set{f&gt;a}\cup E^c</m>.
For a.e. <m>x</m>, <m>\widetilde f(x,\cdot)</m> is measurable (Theorem <xref ref="thm-slice-measurable" text="global"/>) and <m>E_x</m> is measurable (Lemma <xref ref="lem-slice-measurable-set" text="global"/>), so <m>f(x,\cdot)=\widetilde f(x,\cdot)|_{E_x}</m> is measurable; this is (i).
If <m>f\in L(E)</m>, then <m>\widetilde f\in L(\R^{n+m})</m>, and since <m>\int_{\R^m}\widetilde f(x,y)\,dy=\int_{E_x}f(x,y)\,dy</m>, (ii) and (iii) follow from Theorem <xref ref="thm-fubini-1" text="global"/> applied to <m>\widetilde f</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<remark xml:id="rmk-fubini-iterated">
<p>In (iii), the iterated integral on the right-hand side makes sense whenever <m>f\in L(E)</m>, by (i) and (ii).
For nonnegative <m>f</m>, the same formula holds without any integrability assumption; this is Tonelli's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-tonelli" text="global"/>).</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-tonelli">
<title>Tonelli's theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f(x,y)\colon\R^{n+m}\to\overline\R</m> be a nonnegative measurable function.
Then <m>f(x,y)</m> is a measurable function in <m>y</m> for almost every <m>x\in\R^n</m>, the function
<md>x\in\R^n\setminus Z \mapsto \int_{\R^m}f(x,y)\,dy</md>
is measurable for some measure zero set <m>Z</m>, and
<md>\int_{\R^{n+m}} f(x,y)\,dx\,dy = \int_{\R^n}\int_{\R^m} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>f_k=\min\set{f,k}1_{B_k}</m> with <m>B_k=\set{\abs x+\abs y&lt;k}</m>.
Then <m>0\le f_k\le k1_{B_k}</m>, so <m>f_k\in L(\R^{n+m})</m> and Theorem <xref ref="thm-fubini-1" text="global"/> applies to <m>f_k</m>; moreover <m>f_k\nearrow f</m>.
Outside the union <m>Z</m> of the exceptional sets, <m>f(x,\cdot)</m> is measurable (Theorem <xref ref="thm-limsup-measurable" text="global"/>) and <m>\int_{\R^m}f_k(x,y)\,dy\nearrow\int_{\R^m}f(x,y)\,dy</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/>), which is therefore measurable in <m>x</m>.
Applying Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/> in <m>\R^n</m> and in <m>\R^{n+m}</m> to (iii) for <m>f_k</m> gives the identity (both sides may be <m>+\infty</m>).</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<example xml:id="ex-mollifier-monotone">
<title>Exercise V of Homework III</title>
<p>Let <m>u\colon \R^2\to\R</m> be a measurable function.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>Assume that <m>\abs{u}</m> is Riemann integrable on <m>B(0,R) = \set{x\in \R^2 \mid \abs{x}&lt;R}</m> for every <m>R&gt;0</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>For all <m>g\in C_c^\infty(\R^2)</m> with <m>g\ge 0</m>, <m>\int_{\R^2} u \triangle g \ge 0</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
Let <m>\chi\in C_c^\infty(\R^2)</m> be such that <m>0\le \chi\le 1</m>, <m>\chi(x)=0</m> if <m>\abs{x}&gt;1</m>, <m>\chi(x)=\phi(\abs{x})</m> for some <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(\R)</m>, and <m>\int_{\R^2}\chi = 1</m>.
For each <m>k\in\N</m>, let
<md>u_k(x) =
        k^2 \int_{\R^2}u(x-y)\chi(ky)\,dy
        = k^2 \int_{\R^2}u(y)\chi(k(x-y))\,dy \in C^\infty(\R^2).</md>
Show that <m>u_1\ge u_2\ge \cdots</m> and <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty}u_k(x) = u(x)</m> almost everywhere.</p>
</example>
<proof>
<title>Solution</title>
<p>Put <m>\chi_t(x)=t^2\chi(tx)</m> and <m>u_t=u*\chi_t</m> for <m>t&gt;0</m>, so <m>u_k</m> is the case <m>t=k</m>; <m>\chi_t\ge0</m>, <m>\int\chi_t=1</m>, <m>\chi_t=0</m> outside <m>B(0,1/t)</m>.
By (i), <m>u</m> is locally integrable, so <m>u_t</m> is smooth (differentiate under the integral sign).</p>
<p><alert>Step 0.</alert> For smooth <m>w</m>, condition (ii) is equivalent to <m>\triangle w\ge0</m>: integrating by parts, <m>\int w\triangle g=\int(\triangle w)g</m>, and if <m>\triangle w(x_0)&lt;0</m>, a bump <m>g\ge0</m> near <m>x_0</m> makes this negative.</p>
<p><alert>Step 1: if <m>w</m> is smooth with <m>\triangle w\ge0</m>, then <m>t\mapsto(w*\chi_t)(x)</m> is nonincreasing.</alert>
In polar coordinates, <m>(w*\chi_t)(x)=\int_0^1\left(\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}w(x-\tfrac rte^{i\theta})\,d\theta\right)\phi(r)\,r\,dr</m>.
With <m>\rho=r/t</m> and <m>N</m> the outward normal of <m>\pr B(x,\rho)</m>, differentiating under the integral sign and using the divergence theorem,
<md>\frac{\pr}{\pr t}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}w\left(x-\rho e^{i\theta}\right)d\theta
        =-\frac{r}{t^2}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\ags{\nabla w,N}\,d\theta
        =-\frac1t\int_{\pr B(x,\rho)}\ags{\nabla w,N}\,ds
        =-\frac1t\int_{B(x,\rho)}\triangle w\le0.</md>
Since <m>\phi(r)\,r\ge0</m>, the claim follows; for smooth <m>u</m> this already proves <m>u_1\ge u_2\ge\cdots</m>.</p>
<p><alert>Step 2: the general case.</alert>
By Fubini's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-fubini-2" text="global"/>; all integrands have compact support), <m>\int u_k\triangle g=\int\chi_k(y)\int u(z)\triangle g(z+y)\,dz\,dy\ge0</m> for <m>g\ge0</m>, so <m>\triangle u_k\ge0</m> by Step 0.
Again by Fubini, <m>u_k*\chi_m=u_m*\chi_k</m>.
Applying Step 1 to <m>w=u_m</m>,
<md>u_k*\chi_m=u_m*\chi_k\ge u_m*\chi_{k+1}=u_{k+1}*\chi_m.</md>
Since <m>u_k</m> and <m>u_{k+1}</m> are continuous, letting <m>m\to+\infty</m> gives <m>u_k\ge u_{k+1}</m>.</p>
<p>This proves the first claim, <m>u_1\ge u_2\ge\cdots</m>.</p>
<p><alert>The almost everywhere convergence.</alert></p>
<p>Since <m>(u_k(x))_k</m> is nonincreasing, <m>v(x)\coloneqq\lim_{k\to+\infty}u_k(x)\in[-\infty,+\infty)</m> exists for every <m>x</m>, and <m>v</m> is measurable by Theorem <xref ref="thm-limsup-measurable" text="global"/>.
We show that <m>v=u</m> almost everywhere.</p>
<p><em>Step 1: <m>v\in L^1_\mathrm{loc}(\R^2)</m>.</em>
Let <m>K</m> be compact and choose <m>R</m> with <m>K-\overline B(0,1)\subset B(0,R)</m>.
By Fatou's lemma (Theorem <xref ref="thm-fatou-nonnegative" text="global"/>) and Tonelli's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-tonelli" text="global"/>),
<md>\int_K\abs v\le\liminf_{k\to+\infty}\int_K\abs{u_k}
        \le\liminf_{k\to+\infty}\int_{\R^2}\chi_k(y)\left(\int_K\abs{u(x-y)}\,dx\right)dy
        \le\int_{B(0,R)}\abs u&lt;+\infty</md>
by (i).
In particular, <m>v</m> is finite almost everywhere.</p>
<p><em>Step 2: <m>\int vg=\int ug</m> for every bounded measurable <m>g</m> that vanishes outside a compact set <m>K_0</m> and is continuous almost everywhere.</em>
Let <m>M=\sup\abs g</m>.
Since <m>v\le u_k\le u_1</m>, we have <m>\abs{u_kg}\le M(\abs{u_1}+\abs v)1_{K_0}</m>, which is integrable by Step 1, so Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>) gives <m>\int vg=\lim_k\int u_kg</m>.
Substituting <m>y\mapsto y/k</m> and then <m>x\mapsto x+y/k</m>, Fubini's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-fubini-2" text="global"/>) gives
<md>\int_{\R^2}u_kg=\int_{\R^2}\int_{\R^2}u(x)\chi(y)\,g\left(x+\frac yk\right)dx\,dy.</md>
The integrand converges to <m>u(x)\chi(y)g(x)</m> for a.e. <m>x</m> and is dominated by <m>M\abs{u(x)}\chi(y)1_{K_0'}(x)</m>, where <m>K_0'=\set{x\mid\operatorname{dist}(x,K_0)\le1}</m>.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/> again and <m>\int\chi=1</m>, <m>\int vg=\int ug</m>.</p>
<p><em>Step 3: <m>\int_Eu=\int_Ev</m> for every bounded measurable set <m>E</m>.</em>
For a bounded open <m>G=\bigcup_jI_j</m> as in Theorem <xref ref="thm-open-decomposition" text="global"/>, apply Step 2 to <m>g_N=\sum_{j\le N}1_{I_j}</m> (continuous off the null set <m>\bigcup_j\pr I_j</m>) and let <m>N\to+\infty</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>, since <m>g_N\to1_G</m> a.e.
For a bounded <m>G_\delta</m> set <m>H=\bigcap_kG_k</m>, we may take the <m>G_k</m> bounded and decreasing, and Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/> passes to the limit again.
Finally, <m>E=H\setminus Z</m> with <m>H</m> a bounded <m>G_\delta</m> set and <m>\abs Z=0</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-gdelta-fsigma-char" text="global"/>).</p>
<p><em>Step 4: <m>u=v</m> almost everywhere.</em>
Let <m>E_{k,\ell}=\set{\abs x&lt;\ell,\ v(x)&gt;-\infty,\ u(x)&gt;v(x)+\frac1k}</m>, a bounded measurable set on which <m>v</m> is integrable.
By Step 3,
<md>\int_{E_{k,\ell}}v=\int_{E_{k,\ell}}u\ge\int_{E_{k,\ell}}v+\frac1k\abs{E_{k,\ell}},</md>
so <m>\abs{E_{k,\ell}}=0</m>.
Hence <m>\set{u&gt;v}\subset\set{v=-\infty}\cup\bigcup_{k,\ell}E_{k,\ell}</m> is null, and exchanging <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> shows that <m>\set{v&gt;u}</m> is null.
Thus <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty}u_k=u</m> almost everywhere.</p>
</proof>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1020">
<title>20261020</title>
<definition xml:id="def-Lp-space">
<title><m>L^p</m> spaces</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>E</m> be a measurable subset of <m>\R^n</m> and let <m>p&gt;0</m>.
<m>L^p(E)</m> is the set of all measurable functions <m>f\colon E\to\overline\R</m> such that <m>\int_E\abs{f}^p&lt;+\infty</m>.
For <m>f\in L^p(E)</m>, we define the <em>semi-norm</em> of <m>f</m> in <m>L^p(E)</m> by
<md>\norm{f}_p\coloneqq\left(\int_E\abs{f}^p\right)^{1/p}.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>For <m>p=1</m>, this agrees with <m>L(E)=L^1(E)</m> from before, by Theorem <xref ref="thm-absolute-integrability" text="global"/>.
From now on, we assume that <m>1\le p&lt;+\infty</m>.</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-Lp-vector-space">
<statement>
<p>If <m>f,g\in L^p(E)</m>, then <m>f+g\in L^p(E)</m> and <m>cf\in L^p(E)</m> for any constant <m>c</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Clearly <m>\int_E\abs{cf}^p=\abs c^p\int_E\abs f^p</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(iv), <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are finite a.e., so <m>f+g</m> is defined a.e. (set it equal to <m>0</m> on the null set where it is not defined) and measurable.
By convexity of <m>t\mapsto t^p</m>, <m>\abs{a+b}^p\le2^{p-1}(\abs a^p+\abs b^p)</m>, and integrating (Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(ii)) gives <m>\int_E\abs{f+g}^p&lt;+\infty</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>We will prove the following inequality.</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-minkowski">
<title>Minkowski's inequality</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f,g\in L^p(E)</m>.
Then <m>\norm{f+g}_p\le\norm{f}_p+\norm{g}_p</m>.</p>
</statement>
</theorem>
<p>For <m>1&lt;p&lt;+\infty</m>, the <em>conjugate exponent</em> <m>q</m> of <m>p</m> is the number such that <m>1/p+1/q=1</m>, that is, <m>q=p/(p-1)</m>.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-young">
<title>Young's inequality</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>1&lt;p&lt;+\infty</m> and let <m>q</m> be the conjugate exponent of <m>p</m>.
Then for all <m>a,b\ge 0</m>,
<md>ab \le \frac{a^p}{p} + \frac{b^q}{q}.</md>
The equality holds if and only if <m>a^p=b^q</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>The function <m>f(x)=x^p/p+1/q-x</m> on <m>[0,+\infty)</m> has <m>f'(x)=x^{p-1}-1</m>, so it is strictly decreasing on <m>[0,1]</m>, strictly increasing on <m>[1,+\infty)</m>, and <m>f(x)\ge f(1)=0</m> with equality only at <m>x=1</m>.
If <m>b=0</m>, the claim is <m>0\le a^p/p</m>.
If <m>b&gt;0</m>, put <m>x=ab^{-1/(p-1)}</m>; then <m>b^qf(x)=\frac{a^p}{p}+\frac{b^q}{q}-ab\ge0</m>, with equality if and only if <m>a=b^{1/(p-1)}</m>, i.e. <m>a^p=b^q</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-holder">
<title>Hölder's inequality</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>1&lt;p&lt;+\infty</m> and let <m>q</m> be the conjugate exponent of <m>p</m>.
If <m>f\in L^p(E)</m> and <m>g\in L^q(E)</m>, then
<md>\int_E \abs{fg} \le \left(\int_E\abs{f}^p\right)^{1/p}\left(\int_E\abs{g}^q\right)^{1/q}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>A=\norm f_p</m> and <m>B=\norm g_q</m>.
If <m>A=0</m>, then <m>f=0</m> a.e. by Theorem <xref ref="thm-zero-integral" text="global"/>, and both sides vanish; similarly if <m>B=0</m>.
Otherwise, apply Lemma <xref ref="lem-young" text="global"/> with <m>a=\abs{f(x)}/A</m> and <m>b=\abs{g(x)}/B</m> wherever <m>f,g</m> are finite, and integrate:
<md>\frac{1}{AB}\int_E\abs{fg}\le\frac1p+\frac1q=1.</md></p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<proof ref="thm-minkowski">
<p>For <m>p=1</m> this follows from <m>\abs{f+g}\le\abs f+\abs g</m>.
Let <m>1&lt;p&lt;+\infty</m> and <m>q=p/(p-1)</m>.
Since <m>(\abs{f+g}^{p-1})^q=\abs{f+g}^p</m>, we have <m>\abs{f+g}^{p-1}\in L^q(E)</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-Lp-vector-space" text="global"/>, and Lemma <xref ref="lem-holder" text="global"/> gives
<md>\int_E\abs{f+g}^p
        \le\int_E\abs{f+g}^{p-1}\abs f+\int_E\abs{f+g}^{p-1}\abs g
        \le\left(\int_E\abs{f+g}^p\right)^{1/q}\left(\norm f_p+\norm g_p\right).</md>
If <m>\int_E\abs{f+g}^p&gt;0</m>, divide by <m>\left(\int_E\abs{f+g}^p\right)^{1/q}</m> and use <m>1-1/q=1/p</m>.</p>
</proof>
<remark xml:id="rmk-Lp-normed">
<p>Let <m>1\le p&lt;+\infty</m>.
For <m>f,g\in L^p(E)</m> and a constant <m>c</m>, we have <m>\norm{cf}_p=\abs c\norm f_p</m> and <m>\norm{f+g}_p\le\norm f_p+\norm g_p</m> (Minkowski's inequality, Theorem <xref ref="thm-minkowski" text="global"/>), and <m>\norm f_p=0</m> if and only if <m>f=0</m> a.e. in <m>E</m>.
So <m>\norm{\cdot}_p</m> is a norm on <m>L^p(E)</m> once we identify functions that are equal almost everywhere; that is, <m>(L^p(E),\norm{\cdot}_p)</m> is a normed vector space (after quotienting by the relation <q><m>f=g</m> a.e.</q>).</p>
<p>When <m>0&lt;p&lt;1</m>, Minkowski's inequality fails, so <m>(L^p(E),\norm{\cdot}_p)</m> is not a normed vector space.
For example, if <m>\abs E&gt;0</m>, choose disjoint measurable sets <m>A,B\subset E</m> with <m>\abs A=\abs B=c\in(0,+\infty)</m>.
Then <m>\norm{1_A+1_B}_p=(2c)^{1/p}&gt;2c^{1/p}=\norm{1_A}_p+\norm{1_B}_p</m> since <m>1/p&gt;1</m>.
It is only a <em>quasi-normed</em> vector space (Definition <xref ref="def-quasi-normed-space" text="global"/>): <m>\norm{f+g}_p\le2^{1/p-1}\left(\norm f_p+\norm g_p\right)</m>.</p>
<p>When <m>1\le p&lt;+\infty</m>, <m>(L^p(E),\norm{\cdot}_p)</m> is a complete normed vector space, as we will show in Theorem <xref ref="thm-Lp-complete" text="global"/>.
A complete quasi-normed vector space is called a <em>quasi-Banach space</em>.
(In class this was called a Fréchet space; strictly speaking, a Fréchet space is required to be locally convex, which <m>L^p(E)</m> is not for <m>0&lt;p&lt;1</m> and <m>\abs E&gt;0</m>.)</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-Lp-complete">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>1\le p&lt;+\infty</m>.
Let <m>f_k\in L^p(E)</m> for each <m>k\in\N</m>.
Assume that
<md>\lim_{k,\ell\to+\infty} \norm{f_k-f_\ell}_p = 0.</md>
(This is to say that <m>\set{f_k}_{k\in\N}</m> is a Cauchy sequence.)
Then there is an <m>f\in L^p(E)</m> such that <m>\lim_{k\to+\infty}\norm{f_k-f}_p=0</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Changing each <m>f_k</m> on a null set, we may assume the <m>f_k</m> are real-valued (Lemma <xref ref="lem-basic-properties" text="global"/>(iv)).
Choose <m>j_1&lt;j_2&lt;\cdots</m> with <m>\norm{f_\ell-f_m}_p&lt;2^{-k}</m> for <m>\ell,m\ge j_k</m>, and let
<md>g=\abs{f_{j_1}}+\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\abs{f_{j_{k+1}}-f_{j_k}}.</md>
By Minkowski's inequality (Theorem <xref ref="thm-minkowski" text="global"/>) for the partial sums and the monotone convergence theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-monotone-convergence" text="global"/>),
<md>\norm g_p\le\norm{f_{j_1}}_p+\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}2^{-k}&lt;+\infty,</md>
so <m>g&lt;+\infty</m> outside a null set <m>Z</m>.
For <m>x\notin Z</m> the telescoping series converges absolutely, so <m>f(x)\coloneqq\lim_kf_{j_k}(x)</m> exists; put <m>f=0</m> on <m>Z</m>.
Since <m>\abs{f_{j_k}}\le g</m>, we get <m>\abs f\le g</m>, so <m>f\in L^p(E)</m>, and <m>\abs{f_{j_k}-f}^p\le(2g)^p</m> with <m>\abs{f_{j_k}-f}\to0</m> a.e.
Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/> gives <m>\norm{f_{j_k}-f}_p\to0</m>.
Finally, for <m>k,j_\ell\ge N</m> with <m>N</m> from the Cauchy condition,
<md>\norm{f_k-f}_p\le\norm{f_k-f_{j_\ell}}_p+\norm{f_{j_\ell}-f}_p,</md>
which is small for <m>\ell</m> large.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>By Theorem <xref ref="thm-Lp-complete" text="global"/>, for <m>1\le p&lt;+\infty</m> the space <m>L^p(E)</m> (with functions equal almost everywhere identified) is a complete normed vector space, that is, a <em>Banach space</em>.</p>
</subsection>
</section>
<section xml:id="sec-distributions">
<title>Test Functions and Basic Properties of Distributions</title>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-1022">
<title>20261022</title>
<p>We will use the notations <m>\N_0\coloneqq \N\cup\set{0}</m> and <m>\overline\N_0 \coloneqq \N_0\cup\set{+\infty}</m>.
Let <m>X\subset \R^n</m> be an open set.
For every <m>k\in\overline\N_0</m>, let <m>C^k(X)</m> be the space of <m>k</m>-times continuously differentiable functions.
For any multi-index <m>\alpha=(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n)\in \N_0^n</m>, let <m>\abs{\alpha}=\alpha_1+\dots+\alpha_n</m> and
<md>\pr_x^\alpha f = \pr_{x_1}^{\alpha_1}\cdots\pr_{x_n}^{\alpha_n} f
    = \left( \frac{\pr}{\pr x_1} \right)^{\alpha_1}\cdots\left( \frac{\pr}{\pr x_n} \right)^{\alpha_n}f
    = \frac{\pr^{\abs{\alpha}}f}{\pr x_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots \pr x_n^{\alpha_n}}.</md></p>
<definition xml:id="def-support">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\in C^0(X)</m>.
The <em>support</em> of <m>f</m> is the closure in <m>X</m> of the set <m>\set{x\in X\mid f(x)\ne 0}</m> and is denoted by <m>\operatorname{supp} f</m>.
Let <m>k\in\overline\N_0</m>.
If <m>f\in C^k(X)</m> and <m>\operatorname{supp} f</m> is a compact subset of <m>X</m>, we write <m>f\in C_c^k(X)</m>.
If <m>f\in C_c^\infty(X)</m>, we call <m>f</m> a <em>test function</em>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<example xml:id="ex-support">
<p>Let <m>X=\R</m> and
<md>
<mrow>u(x) =
        \begin{cases}
            x(1-x) &amp; \text{if } 0\le x\le 1 \\
            0 &amp; \text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases}</mrow>
</md>
Then <m>u</m> is continuous, <m>\set{u\ne0}=(0,1)</m>, and hence <m>\operatorname{supp} u=[0,1]</m>.
Note that the support is always closed, so it contains the endpoints <m>0</m> and <m>1</m> even though <m>u</m> vanishes there.
Thus <m>u\in C_c^0(\R)</m>.
(But <m>u\notin C^1(\R)</m>, because <m>u'(0^+)=1\ne0=u'(0^-)</m>.)</p>
<image width="60%">
<latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.3) -- (0,1.5) node[above] {$y$};
            \draw[thick, domain=0:1, smooth, variable=\t] plot ({2*\t}, {4*\t*(1-\t)});
            \draw[thick] (-1.2,0) -- (0,0);
            \draw[thick] (2,0) -- (3.2,0);
            \draw[line width=2pt, opacity=0.35] (0,0) -- (2,0);
            \node[below] at (0,-0.05) {$0$};
            \node[below] at (2,-0.05) {$1$};
            \node[above right] at (1,1) {$u$};
            \node[below] at (1,-0.3) {$\operatorname{supp} u=[0,1]$};
        \end{tikzpicture}</latex-image>
</image>
</example>
<lemma xml:id="lem-bump">
<statement>
<p>There is a nonnegative <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(\R^n)</m> with <m>\phi(0)&gt;0</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>f(t)=e^{-1/t}</m> for <m>t&gt;0</m> and <m>f(t)=0</m> for <m>t\le0</m>.
By induction, <m>f^{(k)}(t)=P_k(1/t)e^{-1/t}</m> for <m>t&gt;0</m>, with polynomials <m>P_0=1</m> and <m>P_{k+1}(s)=s^2(P_k(s)-P_k'(s))</m>.
Since <m>s^me^{-s}\to0</m> as <m>s\to+\infty</m>, each <m>f^{(k)}(t)\to0</m> as <m>t\to0^+</m>, and inductively <m>f^{(k)}(0)=0</m>; hence <m>f\in C^\infty(\R)</m>.
Then <m>\phi(x)=f(1-\abs x^2)</m> is smooth, nonnegative, positive exactly on <m>\set{\abs x&lt;1}</m>, and <m>\phi(0)=e^{-1}</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<definition xml:id="def-compactly-contained">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>A,B</m> be subsets of <m>\R^n</m> with <m>A\subset B</m>.
We write <m>A\Subset B</m> if <m>\overline A</m> is a compact subset of <m>B</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-compact-support">
<p>Let <m>f\in C^k(X)</m>.
Then <m>f\in C_c^k(X)</m> if and only if <m>\operatorname{supp} f\Subset X</m>.
Indeed, if <m>\operatorname{supp} f</m> is a compact subset of <m>X</m>, then it is closed in <m>\R^n</m>, so <m>\overline{\operatorname{supp} f}=\operatorname{supp} f</m> is a compact subset of <m>X</m>.
Conversely, if the closure <m>\overline{\operatorname{supp} f}</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is a compact subset of <m>X</m>, then <m>\operatorname{supp} f=\overline{\operatorname{supp} f}\cap X=\overline{\operatorname{supp} f}</m> is compact.</p>
</remark>
<remark xml:id="rmk-scaled-bump">
<p>Let <m>\phi</m> be as in Lemma <xref ref="lem-bump" text="global"/>, and let <m>x_0\in\R^n</m>, <m>\delta&gt;0</m>.
Let <m>F</m> be defined by
<md>F(x) = \phi\left( \frac{x-x_0}{\delta} \right).</md>
Then <m>F\in C_c^\infty(\R^n)</m>, <m>F\ge0</m>, <m>F(x_0)&gt;0</m>, and <m>F</m> has support in the closed ball <m>\overline B_\delta(x_0)</m>, where <m>B_\delta(x_0) = \set{x\in\R^n \mid \abs{x-x_0}&lt;\delta}</m>.
In particular, if <m>x_0\in X</m>, then <m>F\in C_c^\infty(X)</m> for all sufficiently small <m>\delta&gt;0</m>.
(So by translation and scaling we get test functions which are positive at a prescribed point and supported in a prescribed neighborhood of it.)</p>
</remark>
<definition xml:id="def-convolution">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>u,v\in C^0(\R^n)</m>.
If <m>u</m> or <m>v</m> has compact support, then the <em>convolution</em> <m>u\ast v</m> is the function given by
<md>(u\ast v)(x) = \int_{\R^n} u(x-y)v(y)\,dy.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-convolution-continuous">
<p>The substitution <m>y\mapsto x-y</m> shows <m>u\ast v=v\ast u</m>, so we may assume <m>v</m> has compact support; then the integrand is continuous and vanishes outside <m>\operatorname{supp} v</m>, so the integral is well defined.
Moreover, <m>u\ast v</m> is continuous: if <m>x_j\to x</m> with <m>\abs{x_j-x}\le1</m>, then <m>\abs{u(x_j-y)v(y)}\le C1_{\operatorname{supp} v}(y)</m> with <m>C=\sup_{\overline B_1(x)-\operatorname{supp} v}\abs u\cdot\sup\abs v&lt;+\infty</m>, and Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>) applies.
If both <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> have compact support, then <m>\operatorname{supp}(u\ast v)\subset\operatorname{supp} u+\operatorname{supp} v</m>, which is compact.</p>
</remark>
<definition xml:id="def-Lp-loc">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>0&lt;p&lt;+\infty</m>.
Define
<md>L_\mathrm{loc}^p(X) \coloneqq
        \left\lbrace f\colon X\to\overline\R: \text{measurable} \mathrel{\Big\vert} \int_K \abs{f}^p &lt;+\infty \text{ for all } K\Subset X \right\rbrace</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-convolution-Lloc">
<p>For <m>u\in C_c^0(\R^n)</m> and <m>v\in L_\mathrm{loc}^1(\R^n)</m>, we can also define
<md>(u\ast v)(x) = \int_{\R^n}u(x-y)v(y)\,dy.</md>
Again, <m>u\ast v</m> is continuous by Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>: for <m>\abs{x_j-x}\le1</m> the integrands are dominated by <m>\sup\abs u\cdot\abs{v}1_{S}</m>, where <m>S=\overline B_1(x)-\operatorname{supp} u</m> is compact and <m>v\in L^1(S)</m>.</p>
</remark>
<lemma xml:id="lem-convolution-properties">
<statement>
<p><ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>If <m>u,v,w\in C_c^0(\R^n)</m>, then <m>(u\ast v)\ast w = u\ast (v\ast w)</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If <m>u\in C_c^j(\R^n)</m> and <m>v\in C^k(\R^n)</m>, then <m>u\ast v\in C^{j+k}(\R^n)</m> and for all <m>\alpha,\beta\in\N_0^n</m> with <m>\abs{\alpha}\le j</m> and <m>\abs{\beta}\le k</m>, one has <m>\pr_x^\alpha\pr_x^\beta(u\ast v) = (\pr_x^\alpha u)\ast (\pr_x^\beta v)</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If <m>u\in C_c^j(\R^n)</m> and <m>v\in L_\mathrm{loc}^1(\R^n)</m>, then <m>u\ast v\in C^j(\R^n)</m> and <m>\pr_x^\alpha(u\ast v)=(\pr_x^\alpha u)\ast v</m> for all <m>\abs\alpha\le j</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>(i) Both sides equal <m>\int\int u(x-y-z)v(z)w(y)\,dz\,dy</m>: the integrand is continuous and vanishes outside the compact set <m>\operatorname{supp} w\times\operatorname{supp} v</m>, so Fubini's theorem (Theorem <xref ref="thm-fubini-2" text="global"/>) allows exchanging the order of integration after substituting <m>z=s-y</m>.</p>
<p>(iii) Let <m>u\in C_c^1</m> and <m>0&lt;\abs h\le1</m>.
The difference quotient <m>\frac1h\left(u(x+he_i-y)-u(x-y)\right)v(y)</m> vanishes outside the compact set <m>S=\overline B_1(x)-\operatorname{supp} u</m>, is bounded by <m>\sup\abs{\pr_{x_i}u}\cdot\abs{v(y)}</m>, and tends to <m>\pr_{x_i}u(x-y)v(y)</m>.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-dct" text="global"/>, <m>\pr_{x_i}(u\ast v)=(\pr_{x_i}u)\ast v</m>, which is continuous by Remark <xref ref="rmk-convolution-Lloc" text="global"/>.
Iterate, using <m>\pr^\alpha u\in C_c^{j-\abs\alpha}</m>.</p>
<p>(ii) The same argument with the roles of <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> exchanged gives <m>\pr^\beta(u\ast v)=u\ast\pr^\beta v</m> for <m>\abs\beta\le k</m>.
Applying (iii) to <m>u</m> and <m>\pr^\beta v\in C^0\subset L^1_\mathrm{loc}</m> gives <m>\pr^\alpha\pr^\beta(u\ast v)=(\pr^\alpha u)\ast(\pr^\beta v)</m>, which is continuous.
Every <m>\gamma</m> with <m>\abs\gamma\le j+k</m> splits as <m>\alpha+\beta</m> with <m>\abs\alpha\le j</m>, <m>\abs\beta\le k</m>, so <m>u\ast v\in C^{j+k}</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<p>Let <m>\chi\in C_c^\infty(\R^n)</m> be the function such that <m>\chi\ge 0</m>, <m>\operatorname{supp}\chi \subset B_1(0)</m>, and <m>\int_{\R^n}\chi = 1</m>.
(Such a function exists: take <m>\phi</m> from Lemma <xref ref="lem-bump" text="global"/>, put <m>\chi(x)=c\,\phi(2x)</m> with <m>c=\left(\int\phi(2x)\,dx\right)^{-1}</m>; then <m>\operatorname{supp}\chi=\overline B_{1/2}(0)\subset B_1(0)</m>.)
For any <m>\eps&gt;0</m>, let <m>\chi_\eps(x) = \eps^{-n}\chi(x/\eps)\in C_c^\infty(B_\eps(0))</m>.
Note that <m>\int_{\R^n}\chi_\eps(x)\,dx=1</m>.</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-mollification">
<statement>
<p>Fix <m>j\in\N_0</m> and let <m>u\in C_c^j(\R^n)</m>.
Define <m>u_\eps\coloneqq u\ast \chi_\eps\in C_c^\infty(\R^n)</m>.
Then
<md>\lim_{\eps\to 0} \sup\set{\abs{\pr_x^\alpha(u_\eps-u)(x)} \mid x\in \R^n,\, \abs{\alpha}\le j} = 0.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By Lemma <xref ref="lem-convolution-properties" text="global"/>(ii) and Remark <xref ref="rmk-convolution-continuous" text="global"/>, <m>u_\eps\in C_c^\infty(\R^n)</m> and <m>\pr^\alpha u_\eps=(\pr^\alpha u)\ast\chi_\eps</m> for <m>\abs\alpha\le j</m>.
Since <m>\chi_\eps\ge0</m>, <m>\int\chi_\eps=1</m> and <m>\chi_\eps=0</m> outside <m>B_\eps(0)</m>,
<md>\abs{\pr^\alpha(u_\eps-u)(x)}
        =\left\lvert \int_{\R^n}\left(\pr^\alpha u(x-y)-\pr^\alpha u(x)\right)\chi_\eps(y)\,dy \right\rvert
        \le\sup_{\abs y\le\eps}\abs{\pr^\alpha u(x-y)-\pr^\alpha u(x)},</md>
which tends to <m>0</m> uniformly in <m>x</m>, because <m>\pr^\alpha u</m> is continuous with compact support, hence uniformly continuous.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-cutoff">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>K\Subset X</m>.
Then there exists a function <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(X)</m> with <m>0\le\phi\le 1</m> such that <m>\phi\equiv 1</m> in some neighborhood of <m>K</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>K_\delta=\set{x\mid\operatorname{dist}(x,K)&lt;\delta}</m>, and choose <m>\eps&gt;0</m> with <m>\operatorname{dist}(K,\R^n\setminus X)\ge4\eps</m>.
Put <m>\phi=1_{K_{2\eps}}\ast\chi_\eps</m>, which is smooth by Lemma <xref ref="lem-convolution-properties" text="global"/>(iii) and takes values in <m>[0,1]</m>.
Since <m>\chi_\eps=0</m> outside <m>B_\eps(0)</m>: if <m>x\notin K_{3\eps}</m>, then <m>x-y\notin K_{2\eps}</m> for <m>\abs y&lt;\eps</m>, so <m>\phi(x)=0</m>; and if <m>x\in K_\eps</m>, then <m>x-y\in K_{2\eps}</m> for <m>\abs y&lt;\eps</m>, so <m>\phi(x)=\int\chi_\eps=1</m>.
Hence <m>\phi\equiv1</m> on <m>K_\eps</m>, and <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi\subset\overline{K_{3\eps}}</m> is a compact subset of <m>X</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<theorem xml:id="thm-partition-of-unity">
<title>Partition of unity</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>X_1,\dots, X_k</m> be open sets in <m>\R^n</m>.
Then given <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(\bigcup_{j=1}^{k} X_j)</m>, there exists <m>\phi_j\in C_c^\infty(X_j)</m> for each <m>j</m> such that <m>\phi = \phi_1 + \cdots + \phi_k</m>.
If <m>\phi\ge0</m>, we can take all <m>\phi_j\ge0</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By compactness, <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi</m> is covered by finitely many closed balls, each contained in some <m>X_j</m>; let <m>K_j\Subset X_j</m> be the union of those assigned to <m>X_j</m>, so <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi\subset\bigcup_jK_j</m>.
By Theorem <xref ref="thm-cutoff" text="global"/>, take <m>\psi_j\in C_c^\infty(X_j)</m> with <m>0\le\psi_j\le1</m> and <m>\psi_j\equiv1</m> near <m>K_j</m> (<m>\psi_j=0</m> if <m>K_j=\varnothing</m>).
With <m>P_j=(1-\psi_1)\cdots(1-\psi_j)</m> and <m>P_0=1</m>, put
<md>\phi_j=\phi P_{j-1}\psi_j=\phi(P_{j-1}-P_j)\in C_c^\infty(X_j).</md>
The sum telescopes to <m>\phi-\phi P_k</m>, and <m>\phi P_k=0</m> since every point of <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi</m> lies in some <m>K_j</m>, where <m>\psi_j=1</m>.
If <m>\phi\ge0</m>, then each <m>\phi_j\ge0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-distribution">
<statement>
<p>A <em>distribution</em> in <m>X</m> is a linear functional <m>u</m> from the space of test functions <m>C_c^\infty(X)</m> to <m>\C</m> such that for every <m>K\Subset X</m>, there exist constants <m>C&gt;0</m> and <m>k\in\N_0</m> such that
<md>\abs{u(\phi)}
        \le C\sum_{\abs{\alpha}\le k} \sup_{\R^n} \abs{\pr_x^\alpha \phi}</md>
for all <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(X)</m> with <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi\subset K</m>.
Note that <m>C</m> and <m>k</m> depend on <m>K</m>.
The space of all distributions is denoted by <m>\mathcal D'(X)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<example xml:id="ex-L1loc-distribution">
<p>Let <m>u\in L_\mathrm{loc}^1(X)</m>.
Then the linear functional <m>T_u\colon C_c^\infty(X)\to \C</m> defined by
<md>T_u(\phi) = \int_{X} u\phi</md>
is a distribution.
Indeed, <m>T_u</m> is well defined and linear because <m>u\phi</m> is integrable (as <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi\Subset X</m>), and for every <m>K\Subset X</m> and every <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(X)</m> with <m>\operatorname{supp}\phi\subset K</m>,
<md>\abs{T_u(\phi)}\le\int_K\abs u\abs\phi\le\left(\int_K\abs u\right)\sup_{\R^n}\abs\phi.</md>
So the condition holds with <m>C=\max\set{\int_K\abs u,1}</m> and <m>k=0</m>.</p>
</example>
<example xml:id="ex-dirac">
<p>Let <m>p\in X</m>.
The <em>Dirac distribution</em> <m>\delta_p\in\mathcal D'(X)</m> is defined by
<md>
<mrow>\delta_p \colon
        \begin{array}{ccc}
            C_c^\infty(X) &amp; \to &amp; \C \\
            \phi &amp; \mapsto &amp; \phi(p)
        \end{array}.</mrow>
</md>
It is clearly linear, and <m>\abs{\delta_p(\phi)}=\abs{\phi(p)}\le\sup_{\R^n}\abs\phi</m> for all <m>\phi\in C_c^\infty(X)</m>, so <m>\delta_p</m> is a distribution with <m>C=1</m> and <m>k=0</m> for every <m>K\Subset X</m>.
This is important in the theory of PDE.</p>
</example>
</subsection>
</section>
<backmatter>
<appendix xml:id="app-sard">
<title>Sard's Theorem and Applications</title>
<introduction>
<p><em>TA session notes, based on the handout by Shou-Heng Xiang (相守恆).</em></p>
</introduction>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-sard-manifolds">
<title>Manifold and Zero Set</title>
<definition xml:id="def-topological-manifold">
<title>topological manifold</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>M</m> be a topological space. We say <m>M</m> is a <em>topological manifold of dimension <m>n</m></em>, or a <em>topological <m>n</m>-manifold</em>, if it has the following properties:
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p><m>M</m> is Hausdorff.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>M</m> is second-countable, i.e. the topology of <m>M</m> has a countable basis.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>M</m> is locally Euclidean of dimension <m>n</m>: each point of <m>M</m> has a neighborhood <m>U</m> such that there is a homeomorphism <m>\varphi</m> from <m>U</m> onto an open subset of <m>\R^n</m>. We call <m>U</m> a <em>coordinate domain</em>, and <m>\varphi</m> the <em>coordinate map</em>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
The pair <m>(U,\varphi)</m> in the third property is called a <em>coordinate chart</em>. The collection of charts <m>\mathcal M=\set{(U_\alpha,\varphi_\alpha)}_{\alpha\in I}</m> is called an <em>atlas</em> of <m>M</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<example xml:id="ex-manifolds">
<p>The following are topological manifolds.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>The <m>n</m>-dimensional sphere: <m>\mathbb S^n\coloneqq\set{x\in\R^{n+1}\mid \norm{x}=1}</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The real/complex projective space: <m>\F\mathbb P^n\coloneqq \F^{n+1}\setminus\set{0}/{\sim}</m>, where <m>a\sim b\iff a=\lambda b</m> for some <m>\lambda\ne 0</m>, and <m>\F=\R,\C</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The torus: <m>\mathbb T^n\coloneqq(\mathbb S^1)^n</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="exr-topological-manifolds">
<statement>
<p>Check that the three cases in Example <xref ref="ex-manifolds" text="global"/> are topological manifolds.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
<definition xml:id="def-smooth-structure">
<title>smooth structure</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>smooth atlas</em> is an atlas <m>\mathcal M=\set{(U_\alpha,\varphi_\alpha)}_{\alpha\in I}</m> such that
<md>\varphi_\alpha\circ\varphi_\beta^{-1}\colon \varphi_\beta(U_\alpha\cap U_\beta)\to\varphi_\alpha(U_\alpha\cap U_\beta)</md>
is smooth for all <m>\alpha,\beta\in I</m>. A <em>smooth manifold</em> is a topological manifold <m>M</m> which admits a smooth structure, that is, <m>M</m> has a maximal smooth atlas <m>\mathcal M</m>: if <m>\mathcal A</m> is another smooth atlas with <m>\mathcal A\supset\mathcal M</m>, then <m>\mathcal A=\mathcal M</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<exercise xml:id="exr-smooth-manifolds">
<statement>
<p>Check that the three cases in Example <xref ref="ex-manifolds" text="global"/> are smooth manifolds.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
<definition xml:id="def-smooth-map">
<title>smooth map</title>
<statement>
<p>We say <m>F\colon M\to\R</m> is smooth if <m>F\circ\varphi_i^{-1}\colon \varphi_i(U_i)\to\R</m> is smooth for every chart <m>(\varphi_i,U_i)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>Although we cannot define a <q>rectangle</q> in a manifold, we can still define the <em>zero set</em> in a smooth manifold.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-slicing">
<title>Slicing lemma</title>
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>A\subset\R^n</m> is compact and, for every <m>c\in\R</m>, the intersection <m>A\cap(\set{c}\times\R^{n-1})</m> has <m>(n-1)</m>-dimensional measure zero. Then <m>A</m> has <m>n</m>-dimensional measure zero.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>A\subset[a,b]\times\R^{n-1}</m>, <m>A_c=\set{x\mid(c,x)\in A}</m> and <m>\delta&gt;0</m>.
Cover the compact set <m>A_c</m> by finitely many open cubes of total volume <m>&lt;\delta</m>, with union <m>U_c</m>.
By compactness of <m>A</m>, there is an open interval <m>J_c\ni c</m> with <m>A\cap(J_c\times\R^{n-1})\subset J_c\times U_c</m>.
Finitely many <m>J_c</m> cover <m>[a,b]</m>, and they can be chosen with total length <m>\le2(b-a)</m>.
So <m>A</m> is covered by rectangles of total volume <m>&lt;2\delta(b-a)</m>; let <m>\delta\to0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<lemma xml:id="lem-smooth-null-image">
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>A\subset\R^n</m> has measure zero and <m>F\colon A\to\R^n</m> is smooth. Then <m>F(A)</m> has measure zero.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><m>A</m> is covered by countably many compact sets <m>\overline U</m> on which a smooth extension of <m>F</m> satisfies <m>\norm{DF}\le C</m>, hence
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-lipschitz" number="yes">\norm{F(x)-F(x')}\le C\norm{x-x'}.</mrow>
</md>
Cover <m>A\cap\overline U</m> by balls <m>B_j</m> with <m>\sum_j\vol(B_j)&lt;\delta</m>; by <xref ref="eq-lipschitz" text="global"/>, <m>F(A\cap B_j)</m> lies in a ball of <m>C</m> times the radius, so <m>\abs{F(A\cap\overline U)}_e\le C^n\delta</m>.
Let <m>\delta\to0</m> and use Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<p>If <m>M</m> is a smooth <m>n</m>-manifold, with or without boundary, a subset <m>A\subset M</m> is said to have <em>measure zero in <m>M</m></em> if for every smooth chart <m>(U,\varphi)</m>, <m>\varphi(A\cap U)\subset\R^n</m> has <m>n</m>-dimensional measure zero.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-smooth-null-image" text="global"/>, it suffices to check this for the charts of a single atlas.</p>
<remark xml:id="rmk-riemannian-volume">
<p>If <m>M</m> is equipped with a Riemannian metric <mdash/> an inner product on each tangent space <mdash/> we can calculate the volume of <m>M</m> with respect to the metric.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-sard-theorem">
<title>Sard's Theorem and Application</title>
<definition xml:id="def-critical-point">
<title>critical point, critical value</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>F\colon M\to N</m> be a smooth map between smooth manifolds of dimensions <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, respectively. A point <m>p\in M</m> is a <em>critical point</em> of <m>F</m> if the differential
<md>dF_p \colon T_pM \to T_{F(p)}N</md>
is not surjective (equivalently, has rank less than <m>n</m>). A point <m>p\in M</m> that is not critical is called a <em>regular point</em> of <m>F</m>.</p>
<p>A point <m>q\in N</m> is a <em>critical value</em> of <m>F</m> if <m>q=F(p)</m> for some critical point <m>p\in M</m>. A point of <m>N</m> that is not a critical value is called a <em>regular value</em>; in particular, every point of <m>N\setminus F(M)</m> is (vacuously) a regular value.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-critical-coordinates">
<p>In local coordinates, <m>F</m> is represented by a smooth map between open subsets of <m>\R^m</m> and <m>\R^n</m>, and <m>dF_p</m> is represented by the Jacobian matrix <m>\left(\dfrac{\pr F^i}{\pr x^j}(p)\right)</m>; so <m>p</m> is critical exactly when this matrix has rank less than <m>n</m>. When <m>N=\R</m> (so <m>n=1</m>), this says <m>p</m> is critical for <m>g\colon M\to\R</m> exactly when <m>dg_p=0</m>, i.e. all first partial derivatives of <m>g</m> vanish at <m>p</m> <mdash/> this is the sense in which <q><m>a</m> is a regular point of <m>g</m></q> is used in Step 2 of the proof of Theorem <xref ref="thm-sard" text="global"/> below.</p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="thm-sard">
<title>Sard's Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> be smooth manifolds, and let <m>F\colon M\to N</m> be smooth. Then the set of critical values of <m>F</m> has measure zero in <m>N</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let <m>m=\dim M</m> and <m>n=\dim N</m>, and induct on <m>m</m>; for <m>m=0</m>, <m>F(M)</m> is countable.
Using countably many charts (criticality is chart-independent, since transition maps are diffeomorphisms), we may assume <m>F\colon U\to\R^n</m> with <m>U\subset\R^m</m> open.
Let <m>C</m> be the set of critical points and
<md>C_k \coloneqq \set{x\in C \mid \text{all partial derivatives of $F$ of orders $1,\ldots,k$ vanish at $x$}}.</md>
In each step below, it suffices to show that every point has a neighborhood whose relevant image is null, since countably many such neighborhoods cover <m>U</m> (Theorem <xref ref="thm-outer-subadditive" text="global"/>).</p>
<p><alert>Step 1: <m>F(C\setminus C_1)</m> has measure zero.</alert></p>
<aside xml:id="rmk-inverse-function-theorem">
<title>Recall: Inverse Function Theorem</title>
<p>Let <m>G\colon U'\to\R^m</m> be smooth on an open set <m>U'\subset\R^m</m>, and let <m>a\in U'</m>. If the Jacobian matrix <m>DG(a)</m> is invertible, then there are open neighborhoods <m>V\subset U'</m> of <m>a</m> and <m>W</m> of <m>G(a)</m> such that <m>G|_V\colon V\to W</m> is a bijection with smooth inverse.</p>
</aside>
<p>Let <m>a\in C\setminus C_1</m>; after relabeling, <m>\frac{\pr F^1}{\pr x^1}(a)\ne0</m>.
Then <m>G(x)=(F^1(x),x^2,\ldots,x^m)</m> has <m>\det DG(a)=\frac{\pr F^1}{\pr x^1}(a)\ne0</m>, so <m>(u,v)=G(x)</m> are coordinates near <m>a</m>, in which
<md>
<mrow>F(u,v)=\left(u,F^2(u,v),\ldots,F^n(u,v)\right),\qquad
        DF\sim\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\0&amp;B\end{pmatrix},\quad B=\left(\frac{\pr F^i}{\pr v^j}\right)_{i,j\ge2}.</mrow>
</md>
So <m>(c,v)</m> is critical for <m>F</m> if and only if <m>v</m> is critical for <m>F_c(v)=(F^2(c,v),\ldots,F^n(c,v))</m>.
By induction, the critical values of <m>F_c</m> are null in <m>\R^{n-1}</m>, i.e. the critical values of <m>F</m> near <m>a</m> meet each hyperplane <m>\set{c}\times\R^{n-1}</m> in a null set.
Applying Lemma <xref ref="lem-slicing" text="global"/> to compact pieces, they form a null set.</p>
<p><alert>Step 2: <m>F(C_k\setminus C_{k+1})</m> has measure zero for every <m>k</m>.</alert>
Let <m>a\in C_k\setminus C_{k+1}</m>.
Some <m>k</m>-th order partial derivative <m>g</m> of a component of <m>F</m> has <m>dg_a\ne0</m> (Remark <xref ref="rmk-critical-coordinates" text="global"/>), so as in Step 1, <m>Y=g^{-1}(0)\cap V_a</m> is a hypersurface for some neighborhood <m>V_a</m> of <m>a</m>, and <m>C_k\cap V_a\subset Y</m>.
For <m>p\in C_k\cap V_a</m>, <m>dF_p</m> is not surjective, hence neither is <m>d(F|_Y)_p=dF_p|_{T_pY}</m>, so <m>F(C_k\cap V_a)</m> consists of critical values of <m>F|_Y</m>, which are null by induction since <m>\dim Y=m-1</m>.</p>
<p><alert>Step 3: <m>F(C_k)</m> has measure zero if <m>k&gt;m/n-1</m>.</alert>
Let <m>E\subset U</m> be a closed cube of side <m>R</m> and divide it into <m>K^m</m> cubes <m>E_i</m> of side <m>R/K</m>.
If <m>E_i</m> meets <m>C_k</m> at <m>a_i</m>, Taylor's theorem gives <m>\norm{F(x)-F(a_i)}\le A\norm{x-a_i}^{k+1}</m> on <m>E_i</m>, so <m>F(E_i)</m> lies in a ball of radius <m>A'(R/K)^{k+1}</m>.
Hence
<md>\abs{F(C_k\cap E)}_e\le K^m\cdot A''\left(\frac RK\right)^{n(k+1)}=A'''K^{m-n(k+1)}\to0\quad(K\to+\infty).</md></p>
<p>Finally, choosing <m>k&gt;m/n-1</m>, <m>C=(C\setminus C_1)\cup\bigcup_{j=1}^{k-1}(C_j\setminus C_{j+1})\cup C_k</m>, and each piece has null image by Steps 1--3.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<corollary xml:id="cor-sard-low-dimension">
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>F\colon M\to N</m> is smooth and <m>\dim M&lt;\dim N</m>. Then <m>F(M)</m> has measure zero in <m>N</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Every point of <m>M</m> is critical, since <m>dF_p</m> cannot be surjective; apply Theorem <xref ref="thm-sard" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
<corollary xml:id="cor-immersed-null">
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>S\subset M</m> is an immersed submanifold and <m>\dim S&lt;\dim M</m>. Then <m>S</m> has measure zero in <m>M</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Apply Corollary <xref ref="cor-sard-low-dimension" text="global"/> to the inclusion map <m>S\hookrightarrow M</m>.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>
</subsection>
</appendix>
<appendix xml:id="app-fourier">
<title>Introduction to Fourier Series</title>
<introduction>
<p><em>TA session notes, based on the handout by Yu-Zhe Chiang (江宇哲).</em></p>
<p>In this appendix we study Fourier series on the circle and on the unit disk.
The goal is to solve the Laplace equation <m>\Delta u=0</m> and the Poisson equation <m>\Delta u=f</m> on the disk by separation of variables.
Two ingredients are needed.
The first is the basic convergence theory of Fourier series on the circle (Section <xref ref="ssec-fs-circle" text="global"/>).
The second is a characterization of smooth functions on the disk in terms of their Fourier coefficients in the angular variable (Section <xref ref="ssec-fs-disk" text="global"/>).
The latter requires some care because polar coordinates degenerate at the origin.
The two equations are solved in Section <xref ref="ssec-fs-pde" text="global"/>.</p>
</introduction>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-fs-circle">
<title>Fourier series on the circle</title>
<p>We identify a function <m>f</m> on the unit circle <m>S^1=\set{z\in\C\mid\abs z=1}</m> with a <m>2\pi</m>-periodic function on <m>\R</m> via <m>f(x)\coloneqq f(e^{ix})</m>.
Integrals of periodic functions over a period may be taken over any interval of length <m>2\pi</m>.</p>
<definition xml:id="def-fourier-coefficients">
<title>Fourier coefficients, partial sums</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon S^1\to\C</m> be integrable.
The <em><m>n</m>-th Fourier coefficient</em> of <m>f</m> is
<md>f_n \coloneqq \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)e^{-inx}\,dx, \qquad n\in\Z.</md>
The <em><m>m</m>-th partial sum</em> of the Fourier series of <m>f</m> is
<md>S_mf(x) \coloneqq \sum_{\abs n\le m}f_ne^{inx}, \qquad m\in\N.</md>
The <em>sum of the Fourier series</em> of <m>f</m> at <m>x</m>, when the limit exists, is
<md>\widetilde f(x) \coloneqq \lim_{m\to+\infty}S_mf(x).</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>The basic question is: when is <m>\widetilde f=f</m>?
We first put the definitions in the language of inner product spaces.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fs-orthonormal">
<title>Orthonormality</title>
<statement>
<p>For <m>f,g\in L^2(S^1)</m> put
<md>\ags{f,g} \coloneqq \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\overline{g(x)}\,dx,</md>
and let <m>e_n(x)\coloneqq e^{inx}</m>.
Then <m>\ags{e_n,e_k}=\delta_{nk}</m> for all <m>n,k\in\Z</m>.
Moreover, <m>f_n=\ags{f,e_n}</m>, so
<md>S_mf=\sum_{\abs n\le m}\ags{f,e_n}e_n</md>
is the orthogonal projection of <m>f</m> onto the subspace <m>V_m\coloneqq\operatorname{span}\set{e_n\mid\abs n\le m}</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>For <m>n\ne k</m>, <m>e^{i(n-k)x}</m> has the periodic antiderivative <m>e^{i(n-k)x}/(i(n-k))</m>, so <m>\ags{e_n,e_k}=0</m>; clearly <m>\ags{e_n,e_n}=1</m>.
The rest is the definition of <m>f_n</m> and the projection formula for an orthonormal set.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<remark xml:id="rmk-parseval">
<p>The system <m>\set{e_n}_{n\in\Z}</m> is in fact complete: for every <m>f\in L^2(S^1)</m> one has <m>\norm{f-S_mf}_{L^2}\to0</m> and
<md>\sum_{n\in\Z}\abs{f_n}^2=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\abs{f(x)}^2\,dx \qquad\text{(Parseval)}.</md>
We will use this without proof, and only through the following consequence: <em>a continuous function on <m>S^1</m> whose Fourier coefficients all vanish is identically zero.</em>
(Indeed, Parseval gives <m>\int\abs f^2=0</m>, hence <m>f=0</m> a.e. by Theorem <xref ref="thm-zero-integral" text="global"/>, hence <m>f=0</m> by continuity.)</p>
</remark>
<p>To study pointwise convergence, we write <m>S_mf</m> as an integral.</p>
<definition xml:id="def-dirichlet-kernel">
<title>Dirichlet kernel</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>Dirichlet kernel</em> is
<md>D_m(y)\coloneqq\sum_{\abs n\le m}e^{iny}, \qquad m\in\N.</md></p>
</statement>
</definition>
<lemma xml:id="lem-dirichlet-kernel">
<statement>
<p><ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>For <m>y\notin2\pi\Z</m>, <m>\displaystyle D_m(y)=\frac{\sin\left((m+\frac12)y\right)}{\sin\left(\frac y2\right)}</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}D_m(y)\,dy=1</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle S_mf(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(y)D_m(x-y)\,dy</m>; that is, <m>S_mf</m> is the convolution of <m>f</m> with <m>D_m</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>(i) With <m>w=e^{iy}\ne1</m>, the geometric sum is <m>\frac{w^{m+1}-w^{-m}}{w-1}</m>; multiply numerator and denominator by <m>w^{-1/2}</m>.
(ii) Only the term <m>n=0</m> survives.
(iii) Insert the definition of <m>f_n</m> into <m>S_mf</m> and exchange the finite sum with the integral.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<p>We also need the Riemann--Lebesgue lemma: if <m>h\in L^1(a,b)</m>, then <m>\int_a^bh(y)e^{-i\lambda y}\,dy\to0</m> as <m>\lambda\to\pm\infty</m> in <m>\R</m>.
(It is clear for step functions, which are dense in <m>L^1</m>.)</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fs-pointwise">
<title>Pointwise convergence</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon S^1\to\C</m> be a <m>C^1</m> function.
Then <m>S_mf(x)\to f(x)</m> for every <m>x</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Fix <m>x</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-dirichlet-kernel" text="global"/>,
<md>f(x)-S_mf(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{x-\pi}^{x+\pi}g_x(y)\sin\left((m+\tfrac12)(x-y)\right)dy,
        \qquad
        g_x(y)\coloneqq\frac{f(x)-f(y)}{\sin\left(\frac{x-y}{2}\right)}.</md>
Since <m>g_x(y)\to2f'(x)</m> as <m>y\to x</m>, <m>g_x</m> is continuous, hence integrable.
Writing the sine as a combination of <m>e^{\pm i(m+\frac12)(x-y)}</m>, the integral tends to <m>0</m> by the Riemann--Lebesgue lemma.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<remark xml:id="rmk-fs-convergence">
<p>The hypothesis <m>C^1</m> is more than what the proof uses: it is enough that <m>g_x\in L^1</m> (the Dini condition).
Mere continuity is not enough: there are continuous functions whose Fourier series diverge at a point (du Bois-Reymond).
On the other hand, the Cesàro means of <m>S_mf</m> converge uniformly to <m>f</m> for every continuous <m>f</m> (Fejér), and <m>S_mf\to f</m> almost everywhere for <m>f\in L^2</m> (Carleson).
None of this is needed below.</p>
</remark>
<p>Smoothness of <m>f</m> is reflected in the decay of <m>f_n</m>.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fs-decay">
<statement>
<p>If <m>f\in C^k(S^1)</m>, then <m>\abs{f_n}\le\norm{f^{(k)}}_\infty\abs n^{-k}</m> for all <m>n\ne0</m>.
In particular, if <m>f\in C^\infty(S^1)</m>, then <m>f_n</m> decays faster than any power of <m>1/\abs n</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Integrating by parts <m>k</m> times (boundary terms cancel by periodicity), <m>f_n=(in)^{-k}\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f^{(k)}(x)e^{-inx}\,dx</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-fs-disk">
<title>Fourier series on the unit disk</title>
<p>Let <m>\mathbb D=\set{z\in\C\mid\abs z&lt;1}</m>, and write <m>z=re^{i\theta}</m>, <m>x=r\cos\theta</m>, <m>y=r\sin\theta</m>.
For a continuous function <m>f</m> on <m>\mathbb D</m> and <m>m\in\Z</m>, put
<md>f_m(r)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(re^{i\theta})e^{-im\theta}\,d\theta, \qquad 0\le r&lt;1.</md>
That is, <m>f_m(r)</m> is the <m>m</m>-th Fourier coefficient of <m>f</m> restricted to the circle of radius <m>r</m>.
Throughout, <em>smooth</em> means <m>C^\infty</m> in the Cartesian coordinates <m>(x,y)</m>.</p>
<p><alert>Question.</alert> Which conditions on the functions <m>f_m</m> characterize smooth <m>f</m>?</p>
<p>On <m>\mathbb D\setminus\set0</m>, smoothness in <m>(x,y)</m> is the same as smoothness in <m>(r,\theta)</m>.
At the origin, however, the coordinates <m>(r,\theta)</m> degenerate, and smoothness of <m>f</m> there is encoded in the behavior of <m>f_m(r)</m> as <m>r\to0</m>.</p>
<example xml:id="ex-fs-cone">
<p>The function <m>f=x=r\cos\theta=\frac r2(e^{i\theta}+e^{-i\theta})</m> is smooth, with <m>f_{\pm1}(r)=\frac r2</m> and <m>f_m=0</m> otherwise.
The function <m>f=\abs z=r</m> is not smooth at the origin (it is a cone), although <m>f_0(r)=r</m> is smooth on <m>[0,1)</m> and <m>f_m=0</m> for <m>m\ne0</m>.
The difference is that <m>r\ne\varphi(r^2)</m> for any <m>\varphi</m> smooth at <m>0</m>, since this would force <m>\varphi(t)=\sqrt t</m>; see condition (i) of Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-smooth" text="global"/>.</p>
</example>
<p>We use the following two classical facts.</p>
<remark xml:id="rmk-hadamard-whitney">
<title>Recall</title>
<p><alert>(H) Hadamard's lemma.</alert>
If <m>g\in C^\infty((-1,1))</m> and <m>g^{(j)}(0)=0</m> for all <m>j&lt;n</m>, then <m>g(x)=x^nh(x)</m> for some <m>h\in C^\infty((-1,1))</m>.
Indeed, Taylor's formula with integral remainder gives, for <m>n\ge1</m>,
<md>h(x)=\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_0^1(1-s)^{n-1}g^{(n)}(sx)\,ds.</md></p>
<p><alert>(W) Whitney's theorem on even functions.</alert>
If <m>h\in C^\infty((-1,1))</m> is even, then <m>h(x)=\varphi(x^2)</m> for some <m>\varphi\in C^\infty((-1,1))</m>.
(Sketch: let <m>a_k=h^{(2k)}(0)/(2k)!</m> and use Borel's lemma to choose a smooth <m>\psi</m> with <m>\psi^{(k)}(0)=k!\,a_k</m>.
Then <m>h(x)-\psi(x^2)</m> is even and flat at <m>0</m>, so it is a smooth function of <m>x^2</m> as well.)</p>
</remark>
<p>The key observation is that each mode <m>f_m(r)e^{im\theta}</m> is itself a smooth function on <m>\mathbb D</m>.
We obtain it by averaging <m>f</m> over rotations.</p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fs-rotation">
<title>Rotation average</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\in C^\infty(\mathbb D)</m> and <m>m\in\Z</m>.
Define
<md>F_m(z)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(e^{i\alpha}z)e^{-im\alpha}\,d\alpha, \qquad z\in\mathbb D.</md>
Then <m>F_m\in C^\infty(\mathbb D)</m>, <m>F_m(e^{i\beta}z)=e^{im\beta}F_m(z)</m>, and <m>F_m(re^{i\theta})=f_m(r)e^{im\theta}</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Differentiating under the integral sign (the integrand is smooth and the interval compact) gives smoothness.
The substitution <m>\alpha\mapsto\alpha-\beta</m> gives the equivariance, and taking <m>z=r</m>, <m>\beta=\theta</m> gives the last identity.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fs-smooth">
<title>Smoothness criterion</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f\colon\mathbb D\to\C</m> be continuous.
Then <m>f</m> is smooth if and only if the following hold.
<ol marker="(i)">
<li>
<p>(pole condition) For every <m>m\in\Z</m> there is <m>\varphi_m\in C^\infty((-1,1))</m> such that
<md>f_m(r)=r^{\abs m}\varphi_m(r^2), \qquad 0\le r&lt;1.</md></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>(rapid decay) For every <m>r_0\in(0,1)</m> and all integers <m>j,k\ge0</m> there is a constant <m>C=C(r_0,j,k)</m> such that
<md>\abs{\pr_r^jf_m(r)}\le\frac{C}{\abs m^k} \qquad\text{for all } m\ne0,\ 0&lt;r\le r_0.</md></p>
</li>
</ol>
In this case,
<md>f(re^{i\theta})=\sum_{m\in\Z}f_m(r)e^{im\theta}.</md></p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>(<m>\Longrightarrow</m>)
<em>(i)</em> Let <m>n=\abs m</m> and <m>g(x)=F_m(x)</m> on <m>(-1,1)</m>, with <m>F_m</m> from Lemma <xref ref="lem-fs-rotation" text="global"/>.
Then <m>g</m> is smooth, <m>g=f_m</m> on <m>[0,1)</m>, and <m>g(-x)=(-1)^ng(x)</m> (take <m>\beta=\pi</m>).
By equivariance, the Taylor polynomial <m>\sum C_{ab}z^a\overline z^b</m> of <m>F_m</m> at <m>0</m> only has terms with <m>a-b=m</m>, hence <m>a+b\ge n</m>; so <m>g^{(j)}(0)=0</m> for <m>j&lt;n</m>.
By (H) of Recall <xref ref="rmk-hadamard-whitney" text="global"/>, <m>g=x^nh</m> with <m>h</m> smooth and even, and by (W), <m>h(x)=\varphi_m(x^2)</m>.</p>
<p><em>(ii)</em> Integrating by parts <m>k</m> times in <m>\theta</m> as in Lemma <xref ref="lem-fs-decay" text="global"/>,
<md>\pr_r^jf_m(r)=\frac{1}{(im)^k}\cdot\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\pr_\theta^k\pr_r^j\left[f(re^{i\theta})\right]e^{-im\theta}\,d\theta,</md>
and <m>\pr_\theta^k\pr_r^jf</m> is a combination of Cartesian derivatives of order <m>\le j+k</m> with coefficients polynomial in <m>r,\cos\theta,\sin\theta</m>, hence bounded on <m>\set{\abs z\le r_0}</m>.</p>
<p><em>The expansion</em> follows from Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-pointwise" text="global"/> applied to <m>\theta\mapsto f(re^{i\theta})</m>.</p>
<p>(<m>\Longleftarrow</m>)
<em>Step 1: the expansion holds.</em>
By (ii) with <m>k=2</m>, <m>\sum_mf_m(r)e^{im\theta}</m> converges uniformly in <m>\theta</m> to a continuous function with Fourier coefficients <m>f_m(r)</m>, so it equals <m>f(re^{i\theta})</m> by Remark <xref ref="rmk-parseval" text="global"/>.</p>
<p><em>Step 2: each mode <m>u_m(z)=f_m(\abs z)e^{im\theta}</m> is smooth.</em>
By (i), <m>u_m=\varphi_m(\abs z^2)z^m</m> for <m>m\ge0</m> and <m>u_m=\varphi_m(\abs z^2)\overline z^{\abs m}</m> for <m>m&lt;0</m>.</p>
<p><em>Step 3: <m>\sum_m\norm{u_m}_{C^k(K)}&lt;\infty</m> on <m>K=\set{\abs z\le r_0}</m>.</em>
It suffices to consider <m>\abs m\ge2k</m>.
Writing <m>\pr_x,\pr_y</m> in polar coordinates, <m>\pr^\alpha u_m</m> (<m>\abs\alpha\le k</m>) is a sum of terms bounded by <m>C\abs m^kr^{-c}\abs{\pr_r^df_m(r)}</m> with <m>c,d\le k</m>.
By (i), <m>\pr_r^df_m</m> vanishes to order <m>\abs m-d\ge c</m> at <m>0</m>, so Taylor's formula and (ii) give
<md>r^{-c}\abs{\pr_r^df_m(r)}\le\sup_{0&lt;r\le r_0}\abs{\pr_r^{c+d}f_m(r)}\le\frac{C'}{\abs m^{k+2}}.</md>
Hence <m>\abs{\pr^\alpha u_m}\le C''/m^2</m> on <m>K</m>, which is summable.</p>
<p><em>Step 4.</em> By Step 3, <m>\sum_mu_m</m> converges in every <m>C^k(K)</m>, so its sum is smooth, and it equals <m>f</m> by Step 1.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<remark xml:id="rmk-fs-pole-condition">
<p>In (i), only the values of <m>\varphi_m</m> on <m>[0,1)</m> matter, so it is enough that <m>\varphi_m</m> is smooth up to the endpoint <m>0</m>; such a function extends to a smooth function on <m>(-\eps,1)</m>.
Note also that the decay condition (ii) is only required on <m>(0,r_0]</m> with <m>r_0&lt;1</m>, not up to <m>r=1</m>.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-fs-pde">
<title>The Laplace and Poisson equations on the disk</title>
<introduction>
<p>On <m>\mathbb D\setminus\set0</m> the Laplacian is
<md>\Delta=\pr_r^2+\frac1r\pr_r+\frac{1}{r^2}\pr_\theta^2.</md>
For <m>m\in\Z</m> let <m>L_m</m> be the ordinary differential operator
<md>L_m\coloneqq\frac{d^2}{dr^2}+\frac1r\frac{d}{dr}-\frac{m^2}{r^2}.</md></p>
<lemma xml:id="lem-fs-modes">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>u\in C^2(\mathbb D)</m>.
Then <m>(\Delta u)_m(r)=L_mu_m(r)</m> for all <m>m\in\Z</m> and <m>0&lt;r&lt;1</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Differentiate under the integral sign and integrate by parts twice in <m>\theta</m>, using <m>\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\pr_\theta^2u\,e^{-im\theta}\,d\theta=(im)^2\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}u\,e^{-im\theta}\,d\theta</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>
<p>Since <m>L_m(r^\lambda)=(\lambda^2-m^2)r^{\lambda-2}</m>, the general solution of <m>L_mv=0</m> on <m>(0,1)</m> is
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-fs-homogeneous" number="yes">v(r)=Ar^{\abs m}+Br^{-\abs m}\quad(m\ne0),\qquad v(r)=A+B\log r\quad(m=0).</mrow>
</md></p>
</introduction>
<subsubsection>
<title>The Laplace equation</title>
<p>Let <m>g\in C(S^1)</m> with Fourier coefficients <m>g_m</m>.
Note that <m>\abs{g_m}\le\norm g_\infty</m>.</p>
<theorem xml:id="thm-fs-laplace">
<title>Dirichlet problem on the disk</title>
<statement>
<p>Define
<md>u(re^{i\theta})\coloneqq\sum_{m\in\Z}g_mr^{\abs m}e^{im\theta}=g_0+\sum_{m&gt;0}\left(g_mz^m+g_{-m}\overline z^m\right).</md>
Then <m>u\in C^\infty(\mathbb D)</m> and <m>\Delta u=0</m>.
Conversely, if <m>v\in C^2(\mathbb D)\cap C(\overline{\mathbb D})</m> satisfies <m>\Delta v=0</m> in <m>\mathbb D</m> and <m>v=g</m> on <m>\pr\mathbb D</m>, then <m>v=u</m>.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p><em>Existence.</em>
The series converges locally uniformly, and <m>u_m(r)=g_mr^{\abs m}</m>.
This satisfies (i) of Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-smooth" text="global"/> with <m>\varphi_m\equiv g_m</m>, and (ii) because <m>\abs{\pr_r^j(g_mr^{\abs m})}\le\norm g_\infty\abs m^jr_0^{\abs m-j}</m> decays exponentially in <m>\abs m</m>.
So <m>u</m> is smooth, and <m>(\Delta u)_m=L_m(g_mr^{\abs m})=0</m> by Lemma <xref ref="lem-fs-modes" text="global"/>; hence <m>\Delta u=0</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-pointwise" text="global"/>.</p>
<p><em>Uniqueness.</em>
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-fs-modes" text="global"/>, <m>v_m</m> solves <m>L_mv_m=0</m>, so it has the form <xref ref="eq-fs-homogeneous" text="global"/>.
Boundedness of <m>v</m> forces <m>B=0</m>, and uniform continuity on <m>\overline{\mathbb D}</m> gives <m>v_m(r)\to g_m</m> as <m>r\to1</m>, so <m>A=g_m</m>.
Thus <m>v_m=u_m</m> for all <m>m</m>, and <m>v=u</m> by Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-pointwise" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<definition xml:id="def-poisson-kernel">
<title>Poisson kernel</title>
<statement>
<p>For <m>0\le r&lt;1</m> the <em>Poisson kernel</em> is <m>P_r(\theta)\coloneqq\sum_{m\in\Z}r^{\abs m}e^{im\theta}</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>Put <m>z=re^{i\theta}</m>.
Summing two geometric series,
<md>P_r(\theta)=1+\frac{z}{1-z}+\frac{\overline z}{1-\overline z}=\operatorname{Re}\left(\frac{1+z}{1-z}\right)=\frac{1-r^2}{1-2r\cos\theta+r^2}.</md>
Inserting <m>g_m=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int g(\tau)e^{-im\tau}\,d\tau</m> into the formula for <m>u</m> in Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-laplace" text="global"/> and exchanging sum and integral (the series converges uniformly for fixed <m>r&lt;1</m>), we obtain the <em>Poisson integral formula</em>
<md>u(re^{i\theta})=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}g(\tau)P_r(\theta-\tau)\,d\tau.</md></p>
<remark xml:id="rmk-poisson-boundary">
<p>Since <m>P_r&gt;0</m>, <m>\frac1{2\pi}\int P_r=1</m>, and <m>P_r</m> concentrates near <m>\theta=0</m> as <m>r\to1</m>, the kernel is an approximate identity, and one shows that <m>u(re^{i\theta})\to g(\theta)</m> uniformly as <m>r\to1</m>.
So <m>u</m> does attain the boundary values <m>g</m>.
We do not reproduce this standard argument.</p>
</remark>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection>
<title>The Poisson equation</title>
<p>Now consider <m>\Delta u=f</m> in <m>\mathbb D</m> and <m>u=g</m> on <m>\pr\mathbb D</m>, with <m>f\in C(\overline{\mathbb D})</m> and <m>g\in C(S^1)</m>.
By Lemma <xref ref="lem-fs-modes" text="global"/>, the coefficients of a solution satisfy, for each <m>m\in\Z</m>,
<md>L_mu_m=f_m\quad\text{on }(0,1),\qquad u_m(1)=g_m,\qquad u_m\text{ bounded near }0.</md>
The singular homogeneous solutions <m>r^{-\abs m}</m> and <m>\log r</m> are excluded by boundedness, exactly as before.
A particular solution vanishing at <m>r=1</m> is given by variation of constants.
Write <m>r_&lt;=\min\set{r,s}</m> and <m>r_&gt;=\max\set{r,s}</m>, and put
<md>K_m(r,s)\coloneqq\frac{s}{2\abs m}\,r_&lt;^{\abs m}\left(r_&gt;^{\abs m}-r_&gt;^{-\abs m}\right)\ (m\ne0),\qquad K_0(r,s)\coloneqq s\log r_&gt;.</md></p>
<proposition xml:id="prop-fs-poisson">
<statement>
<p>If <m>u\in C^2(\mathbb D)\cap C(\overline{\mathbb D})</m> solves <m>\Delta u=f</m> in <m>\mathbb D</m> and <m>u=g</m> on <m>\pr\mathbb D</m>, then for every <m>m\in\Z</m> and <m>0&lt;r&lt;1</m>,
<md>u_m(r)=g_mr^{\abs m}+\int_0^1K_m(r,s)f_m(s)\,ds\quad(m\ne0),\qquad
        u_0(r)=g_0+\int_0^1K_0(r,s)f_0(s)\,ds.</md>
In particular, <m>u</m> is unique.</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<title>Sketch of proof</title>
<p>The function <m>p_m(r)=\int_0^1K_m(r,s)f_m(s)\,ds</m> satisfies <m>L_mp_m=f_m</m> (differentiate twice), <m>p_m(1)=0</m>, and is bounded near <m>0</m>.
So <m>u_m-p_m</m> is a bounded solution of <m>L_mv=0</m> tending to <m>g_m</m> at <m>r=1</m>, i.e. <m>g_mr^{\abs m}</m> by <xref ref="eq-fs-homogeneous" text="global"/>.
Uniqueness follows as in Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-laplace" text="global"/>.</p>
</proof>
</proposition>
<remark xml:id="rmk-fs-regularity">
<p>For smooth <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, one expects (and elliptic regularity confirms) that the solution is smooth, so that the functions above are exactly its Fourier modes.
One could also verify directly that they satisfy conditions (i) and (ii) of Theorem <xref ref="thm-fs-smooth" text="global"/>.
We omit this.</p>
</remark>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>
</appendix>
</backmatter>
</article>
</pretext>
